\section{Set-up and inference method} % (fold)
\label{sec:set_up}


We are interested in a parameter $\theta_0 \in \R^d$ we would like to make inference on, using an estimator $\widehat{\theta}$. This estimator is based on the observed random variables $(W_{ij\ell})_{1\le i\le C_1,1\le j\le C_2, 1 \le \ell \le N_{ij}}$. While index $\ell$ refers to the units of interest (e.g., individuals or firms), the two indices $i$ and $j$ correspond to the two dimensions of ``clustering'', with a dependence structure that will be clarified below. For instance, these dimensions may correspond to industries and geographical areas. We allow the number $N_{ij}$ of observed units in ``cell'' $(i,j)$ to vary, and also to be zero, in which case we do not observe any unit in $(i,j)$. Hereafter, we let $W_{ij}:=((W_{ij\ell})_{1\le \ell \le N_{ij}}, N_{ij})$ denote all the observed variables attached to cell $(i,j)$.
% We also maintain four assumptions here. The first three assumptions are introduced right below. The fourth will appear after we present our inference method.

\medskip
Hereafter, we mostly consider tests of nominal level $\alpha\in (0,1)$ of the null hypothesis that $\theta_0 = \theta$, against $\theta_0\ne \theta$; we also briefly discuss unilateral tests when $d=1$, as well as confidence intervals and confidence regions. We base our inference method on the asymptotic linearity of $\widehat{\theta}$. Specifically, we assume that for some function $f$ and $Y_{ij}:=f(W_{ij})$,
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\theta}-\theta_0 \simeq \overline{Y},	
	\label{eq:approx_lin}
\end{equation}
where, for any random array $(D_{ij})_{1\le i\le C_1, 2\le j\le C_2}$, $\overline{D}=(C_1C_2)^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{C_1}\sum_{j=1}^{C_2} D_{ij}$. The exact condition corresponding to \eqref{eq:approx_lin} will be given in Assumption \ref{as:as_lin} below. We also assume that we can estimate $Y_{ij}$ by $\widehat{Y}_{ij}:=\widehat{f}(W_{ij})$ and that roughly speaking, the estimation of $Y_{ij}$ does not affect inference; Assumption \ref{as:consistent_V} below formalizes this idea.

\begin{example}
Suppose that $\widehat{\theta}$ is an OLS estimator of $A_{ij}$ on $X_{ij}$. Since
$$\widehat{\theta}-\theta_0 = \overline{XX'}^{-1} \overline{X(A - X'\theta_0)},$$
we can expect that \eqref{eq:approx_lin} holds with $Y_{ij}=E[XX']^{-1}X_{ij}(A_{ij} - X_{ij}'\theta_0)$. We then estimate $Y_{ij}$ by $\widehat{Y}_{ij}:=\overline{XX'}^{-1}X_{ij}(A_{ij} - X_{ij}'\widehat{\theta})$.
\end{example}

\medskip
We now define our tests. As those proposed by MH and CGM, they rely on the following three variance estimators:
\begin{align}
		\widehat{V}_1 & = \frac{1}{C_1^2}\sum_{i=1}^{C_1}\left(\frac{1}{C_2}\sum_{j=1}^{C_2}\widehat{Y}_{ij} - \overline{\widehat{Y}} \right)^{\otimes 2}, \notag \\
		\widehat{V}_2 & = \frac{1}{C_2^2}\sum_{j=1}^{C_2}\left(\frac{1}{C_1}\sum_{i=1}^{C_1} \widehat{Y}_{ij} - \overline{\widehat{Y}} \right)^{\otimes 2}, \label{eq:def_Vhat} \\
		\widehat{V}_{12} & = \frac{1}{(C_1C_2)^2}\sum_{i=1}^{C_1}\sum_{j=1}^{C_2} \left(\widehat{Y}_{ij} - \overline{\widehat{Y}}\right)^{\otimes 2}, \notag 	
\end{align}
where, for any matrix $A$, $A^{\otimes 2}:=AA'$. For simplicity, and since they do not matter asymptotically, we do not consider the degrees-of-freedom corrections suggested by CGM. 


\subsubsection*{Univariate tests} % (fold)
\label{ssub:univariate_tests}
We first present the test proposed by MH and CGM when $\theta_0\in\mathbb{R}$. Let $\widehat{V}_u:=\widehat{V}_1+\widehat{V}_2-\widehat{V}_{12}$, where the index ``u'' refers to ``usual''.
Then, MH and CGM consider the test $\phi_{u,\alpha} = \ind{|t_u|>z_{1-\alpha/2}}$, where $z_{1-\alpha/2}$ is the quantile of order $1-\alpha/2$ of a standard normal distribution and
$$t_u = \frac{\widehat{\theta}-\theta}{\widehat{V}_u^{1/2}}.$$
This approach has two important, related drawbacks. First, $\widehat{V}_u$  can be negative, in which case the test above is not defined. Second, this test  may not be asymptotically valid, as \cite{menzel2021bootstrap} showed in an example for which $P(\widehat{V}_u<0)\to 39.3\%$. To solve these issues, we propose a simple modification. Specifically, let $\se_k=\widehat{V}_k^{1/2}$ for $k\in\{1,2\}$, $\se_u=\max(0,\widehat{V}_u)^{1/2}$  and let $\se=\max(\se_1,\se_2,\se_u)$. Then, consider the test $\phi_{\alpha} = \ind{|t|>z_{1-\alpha/2}}$, where
$$t = \frac{\widehat{\theta}-\theta}{\se}.$$
Remark that we simply replace the usual standard error $\se_u$ with the maximum of $\se_u$, $ \se_1$ and $\se_2$. An intuition behind this  test is that the second (resp., the first) dimension of clustering may not matter. In such a case, it would be more natural to consider $\se_1$ (resp. $\se_2$) rather than $\se_u$. We then take a conservative approach by picking the maximum of these three standard errors. It turns out, however, that when $\widehat{\theta}$ is asymptotically Gaussian, our test is not asymptotically conservative. Our test is (potentially) conservative only in non-Gaussian cases, for which  $\phi_{u,\alpha}$ may be asymptotically invalid. 

\medskip
Two additional remarks are in order. First, in the rest of paper, we do not discuss unilateral tests nor confidence intervals on $\theta_0$ (given by $[\widehat{\theta} \pm \, z_{1-\alpha/2} \, \se]$), but our results on bilateral tests below directly extend to them. Second, CGM consider another fix to possibly negative $V_u$. Assume that $\widehat{\theta}$ is a component of a vector $\widehat{\beta}$ (e.g., $\widehat{\beta}$ is an OLS estimator of a multivariate regresion and $\widehat{\theta}$ is a specific coefficient). Let $\widetilde{V}_u$ denote the variance estimator of $\widehat{\beta}$. Then, if $\widetilde{V}_u$ is not symmetric positive semidefinite, consider the eigendecomposition of $\widetilde{V}_u$, $P'\Delta P$, and replace the negative eigenvalues in $\Delta$ by 0. Note that if $\widehat{\theta}=\widehat{\beta}$, this simply amounts to replacing $\widehat{V}_u$ by $\max(0,\widehat{V}_u)$. The issue with this fix is twofold. First, it does not restore valid inference when the usual approach fails. Second, the corresponding inference is not invariant to affine reparametrization of covariates. For instance, we show in Subsection \ref{sub:lin_reg} that adding a constant or changing the scale of a regressor can make the rejection rate vary from 0 to 1. Similarly, changing the reference of a binary regressor affects inference. 

% subsubsection univariate_tests (end)

\subsubsection*{Multivariate tests} % (fold)
\label{ssub:multivariate_tests}

If $\theta_0\in \mathbb{R}^d$ with $d>1$, we consider the ``usual'' test\footnote{Neither MH nor CGM consider such a test, but we label it ``usual'' as it seems to be the natural multivariate counterpart of the univariate test they consider.} $\phi_{u,\alpha} = \ind{\widetilde{F}_u>q_{1-\alpha/2}(d)}$, where $q_{1-\alpha}(d)$ is the quantile of order $1-\alpha$ of a $\chi^2(d)$ and
	$$\widetilde{F}_u = \ind{F_u<0} \times \infty + \ind{F_u\ge 0} \times F_u, \quad \text{with } F_u= (\widehat{\theta}-\theta)' \widehat{V}^{-1}_u (\widehat{\theta}-\theta).$$
Here we take the convention that $0\times \infty=0$. Thus, when $F_u<0$, $\widetilde{F}_u=\infty$ and we reject the test, as we do in the univariate case when $\widehat{V}_u<0$.

\medskip
We consider the following modification of this F-test. Let us first assume that $\widehat{V}_1$, $\widehat{V}_2$ and $\widehat{V}_u$ are invertible; the case where one of them is singular is considered below. For $k\in\{1,2\}$, let
	$$F_k = (\widehat{\theta}-\theta)' \widehat{V}_k^{-1} (\widehat{\theta}-\theta),$$
	while $F_u$ is still defined as above. Note that by construction, $F_k\ge 0$ for $k\in\{1,2\}$. Then, our test is $\phi_{\alpha} = \ind{F>q_{1-\alpha}(d)}$ where
	\begin{equation}
		F = \min\left(\widetilde{F}_u, F_1, F_2\right).	
		\label{eq:def_F}
	\end{equation}
As above, we thus take a conservative approach by picking the minimum between three F-statistics, the usual one (set to $\infty$ if negative) and two others obtained by focusing on one dimension of clustering only.

\medskip
Now, if $\widehat{V}_1$, $\widehat{V}_2$ or $\widehat{V}_u$ is singular, let us define
$$F^\lambda_k = (\widehat{\theta}-\theta)' \left(\lambda \Id +\widehat{V}_k\right)^{-1} (\widehat{\theta}-\theta), \quad k\in\{1,2,u\},$$
where $\Id$ denotes the identity matrix. As above, let $\widetilde{F}^\lambda_u=\ind{F^\lambda_u<0} \times \infty + \ind{F^\lambda_u\ge 0} \times F^\lambda_u$.  Then, our test is $\phi_{\alpha} = \ind{F>q_{1-\alpha}(d)}$ where
$$F = \lim_{\lambda \downarrow 0} \min\{\widetilde{F}^\lambda_u, F^\lambda_1, F^\lambda_2\}.$$
A simple alternative would be to define $F$ as above, replacing inverses by Moore-Penrose inverses. However, this could lead to conservative inference in some cases where our approach leads to asymptotically non-conservative inference.%\textcolor{red}{To clarify a little bit}
\footnote{The reason behind is that for any symmetric positive semidefinite matrices $A$ and $B$, $A\gg B$ (meaning that $A-B$ is symmetric positive semidefinite) does not imply that their Moore-Penrose inverses $A^+$ and $B^+$ satisfy  $B^+\gg A^+$. On the other hand, for all $\lambda>0$, we do have $(\lambda \Id + B)^{-1}\gg (\lambda \Id + A)^{-1}$.}

\medskip
The test above does not rely on a single F-statistic. As a result, the construction of confidence regions on $\theta_0$ is more complex than usual. We relegate this discussion to Appendix \ref{sec:CR_multiv}.

\medskip
We also consider multivariate tests based on a Bonferroni correction of univariate tests. Albeit conservative in general, these tests turn out to rely on milder restrictions than the tests above. They also lead to straightforward rectangular confidence regions. Let $\widehat{\theta}=(\widehat{\theta}_1,...,\widehat{\theta}_d)'$, $\theta=(\theta_1,...,\theta_d)'$ and let $\se^\ell_k$ denote the square root of the $\ell$-th diagonal term of $\widehat{V}_k$ for $k\in\{1,2,u\}$. As before, we take the convention that $\se^\ell_u=0$ if the $\ell$-th diagonal term of $\widehat{V}_u$ is negative. Let $\se^\ell=\max\{\se^\ell_1,\se^\ell_2,\se^\ell_u\}$ and $t^\ell:=(\widehat{\theta}_\ell- \theta_\ell)/\se^\ell$. Then, the Bonferroni-based test of $H_0$ is
$$\phi^b_\alpha :=  \max_{\ell=1,...,d} \ind{|t^\ell| > z_{1-\frac{\alpha}{2d}}}.$$

