\begin{abstract}
We formulate a spatial extension of the Leggett-Garg inequality by considering three distant observers locally measuring a many-body system at three subsequent times. 
The spatial form, in particular, is specially suited to analyze propagation of quantum perturbations through spin chains, by capturing how a measurement at one site can later affect distant sites due to the interactions. 
We illustrate our proposal for a Heisenberg chain in a magnetic field, showing indeed that the first inequality-violation time scales proportionally to the distance between measuring parties.
We attribute this phenomenon to Lieb-Robinson physics and, confirming this connection, we find that violations are anticipated when increasing the interaction range.
The inequality violation, readily observable in current experiments, demonstrates the incompatibility between two-point correlation functions and the macrorealistic hypothesis. 
In outlook, spatial Leggett-Garg inequalities constitute a new tool for analyzing the non-relativistic dynamics of many-body quantum systems.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle  

The Leggett-Garg inequality (LGI) \cite{leggettgarg1985} provides a testable criterion to analyze the correlations in time of an evolving physical system.
Its formulation assumes intuitive classical concepts, like macroscopic realism and noninvasive measurability \cite{emary2014, vitagliano2023}, yielding constraints on the statistics of observations at multiple times. 
The first LGI violation by quantum systems was observed experimentally in 2010 \cite{palacios2010experimental}.
This evidence, together with a long series of subsequent experiments (see the review \cite{emary2014} and references therein), requires us to adopt quantum mechanics as the everyday working tool for correctly describing correlations in microscopic systems.
In the last decades, LGIs have been extended to various scenarios, including multiple measurements and times \cite{avis2010, emary2014, budroni2013bounding}, macroscopic spin-$j$ systems (with $j$ large) \cite{kofler2008}, implementations in locally-measured open many-body systems  \cite{mendoza2019enhancing}, and its loopholes have been addressed \cite{wilde2012addressing, budroni2015quantum}.
We note that, so far, the LGIs have been used to characterize the time correlations of the same local measurements performed on either single or many-body systems.
Including instead space, for instance by measuring different parts of a time-evolving many-body system, could allow probing the non-relativistic spreading of quantum correlations. 


In this Letter, we obtain the Leggett-Garg inequality for a protocol consisting of multiple parties performing spatially-separated sequential measurements (see Fig.~\ref{fig-scheme}).
Our conceptual extension is general and in principle applicable to physical systems in any dimension and ruled by any Hamiltonian.
For simplicity, with the aim of devising an experimentally-testable scenario, we analyze the Leggett-Garg correlator for a (spatio-temporally) symmetric measurement protocol performed on a spin chain evolving under the Heisenberg Hamiltonian in a magnetic field \cite{arnesen2001natural, amico2008entanglement}. 
For this model, we first demonstrate that interactions are necessary to induce violation of the spatial LGI. We then observe the damping of the LGI violation when increasing the system size, showing that the propagation of a quantum signal through the chain is increasingly difficult for larger distances. Along this line, since time is necessary to establish correlations, the first instance of violation takes place at times increasing with the system size. This is coherent with the fact that quantum correlations can be distributed in spin chains with at most finite velocity \cite{cheneau2012light}, theoretically limited by the Lieb-Robinson bound~\cite{lieb1972}. As an application, the spatial Leggett-Garg experiment can allow reconstructing the light cone governing quantum correlation spreading in spin chains. 

The major objective of our work is to provide a concrete proposal for the experimental observation of spatial LGI violations in spin chains.  
Suitable platforms are, e.g., Rydberg atoms \cite{browaeys2020many, PRXQuantum.3.020303, geier2021floquet, miller2024two}, trapped atoms \cite{bloch2012quantum, gross2017, doi:10.1126/science.abk2397}, ions \cite{blatt2012quantum, RevModPhys.93.025001}, and ultra-cold atoms \cite{jepsen2020spin, PhysRevX.11.011011}. 
Observing the violations will demonstrate that the propagation of correlations in spin chains is incompatible with macrorealism. 


