The original formulation of the Leggett-Garg inequality considers an observer performing the binary measurement $Q$ on a system at the three subsequent times: $t_A$, $t_B$ and $t_C$. Next, one introduces the two-times correlation functions $c_{X,Y} =\langle Q(t_X) Q(t_Y) \rangle = \sum_{\{q_X,q_Y\} = \pm 1} q_X q_Y \, P_{q_Xq_Y}(Q,t_X,t_Y)$, 
with $P_{xy}(Q,t_X,t_Y)$ being the joint probabilities of measuring the dichotomic observable $Q$ at time $t_X$ and getting $q_X$, and again $Q$ at time $t_Y$ and getting $q_Y$. It can be shown that the quantity $K = c_{A,B} + c_{B,C} - c_{A,C}$ satisfies the Leggett-Garg inequality $-3 \leq K\leq 1$ for a system respecting the macrorealistic assumptions, for instance, non-invasive measurability or the fact that measurement does not affect the subsequent dynamics~\cite{vitagliano2023}. The upper-bound value $K=1$, in particular, can be derived by considering the combinations that the value that $Q$ takes in the possible classical scenarios and evaluating the correlators \cite{emary2014}.
Quantum dynamics violates the Leggett-Garg inequality, as can be seen already in simple two-level single-particle systems. 
A clear example is a spin-$1/2$ initialized in the state $\ket{+}$, with $\sigma^x \ket{+} = \ket{+}$ and $\sigma^{x,y,z}$ the Pauli matrices, and evolving under the Hamiltonian $\hat{H} = -h \sigma^z/2$. 
For three $\sigma^x$ measurements at times $t_A = 0$, $t_B = t$, $t_C = 2t$, one obtains $K = 2 \cos(h t) - \cos (2h t)$, which yields the maximal inequality violation $K=3/2$ at times $t = \pi/(3h) + 2p\pi/h$ and $t = 5\pi/(3h) + 2p\pi/h$, where $p$ is a generic integer  \cite{kofler2008}.

Let us now consider a many-body system to conceptually extend the Leggett-Garg inequality in space.
For concreteness, see Fig.~\ref{fig-scheme}, we analyze a one-dimensional spin chain made of $N$ two-level systems.
We consider the three observers $A$, $B$, and $C$, each one measuring the same observable at their respective chain sites $1$, $n$, and $N$, respectively at times $t_{A}$, $t_{B}$, and $t_{C}$.
For simplicity, we will analyze the spatially-symmetric configuration corresponding to $N = 2n-1$, and simply take $t_A = 0$, $t_B = t$, and $t_C = 2t$.
Note that, in this way, $n-1$ labels the lattice distance between the measuring parties: for $n=1$ all parties coincide, then $n=2$ corresponds to three adjacent parties, $n=3$ corresponds to three parties with one spin in between, and so on.
It is easy to generalize the above simplifications, as well as to relax the assumptions on the spatial dimension, boundary conditions, chain length, and number of levels.

To derive the spatial Leggett-Garg inequality, we delocalize the measurements in space and redefine the correlation functions as $C_{X,Y} = \langle Q_{X}(t_X) Q_Y(t_Y) \rangle$, which describes the sequential expectation value of measuring $Q$ at site $X$ and time $t_X$ and later at time $t_Y$ we measure particle $Y$. We define the spatial extension of the Leggett-Garg inequality as 
\begin{equation}
-3 \leq K_n := C_{1,n} + C_{n,2n-1} - C_{1,2n-1}\leq 1.
\label{LGspatial}
\end{equation}
Let us discuss Eq.~\eqref{LGspatial} in detail. For $n=1$, it represents the ordinary LGI for a single spin. For $n>1$, violation would indicate incompatibility with macrorealism in the extended system due to e.g. invasive measurements across the system. Thus, Eq.~\eqref{LGspatial} is specially suited to test propagation of correlations through the chain by capturing how the measurement in one site affects distant sites at later times.    

In order to analyze the quantum dynamics of $K_n$ in a concrete case, we need to specify the system Hamiltonian, the initial state, and which measurement is performed.
To begin with, we consider the isotropic Heisenberg chain in a magnetic field with open boundaries: 
\begin{equation}
H = J \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \vec{\sigma}_i \cdot \vec{\sigma}_{i+1} 
- \frac{h}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{N} %\left( 
\sigma^z_i, %+ \mathbbm{1}_i 
%\right), 
\label{XXHamiltonian}
\end{equation}
with $\vec{\sigma}_i = (\sigma^x_i, \sigma^y_i, \sigma^z_i)^T$ and $J$, $h$ being the coupling constants.
As initial state, we take the product state $\ket{\Psi} =  \ket{+}^{\otimes N}$. Finally, the quantum correlators involved in the inequality are then readily computed as $C_{X,Y} = \Re{\bra{\Psi}Q_X(t_X)Q_Y(t_Y)\ket{\Psi}}$~\cite{Fritz2010}.




The value of $K_n$ can be calculated analytically in the simple case of $J=0$. 
In this case, the result does not depend on $n$ since all spins evolve independently under the local Hamiltonian $\propto \sigma^z$, and thus rotate in the $xy$ plane under the action of the external field $h$. 
If the parties respectively measure the observables $\sigma_1^x$, $\sigma_{n}^x (t)$, and $\sigma_{2n-1}^x (2t)$, where the lower index indicates the chain site of the measurement, we obtain $K_n = \left[ 3 \cos(h t) - 2 \cos(2h t) + \cos(3h t)  \right]/2$ (see App.~\ref{appA}). 
This function, depicted as the black solid line in Fig.~\ref{LG_spatial}, does not lead to any inequality violation. 
This result suggests us that, to observe LGI violations, the interactions between the spins are necessary. Indeed, beyond this example, for non-interacting Hamiltonians we have $[Q_X(t_X),Q_Y(t_Y)] = 0$  for $X\neq Y$ and $X, Y\in \{A,B,C\}$. Under only this assumption, one can prove that the inequality Eq.~\eqref{LGspatial} cannot be violated (see App.~\ref{appA}). Next, by turning on the interaction, we observe violation in the spatial LGI as demonstrated in Fig.~\ref{LG_spatial}. 


\begin{figure}[t!]
%\centering
\includegraphics[width=.99\linewidth]{fig2.pdf}

\caption{Dynamics of the spatial Leggett-Garg inequality Eq.~\eqref{LGspatial} in the spin chain Eq.~\eqref{XXHamiltonian} with $J=1$ and $h =1$ for various distances $n$ between measuring parties, and $Q = \sigma^x$. 
The black solid curve represents the noninteracting case of $J=0$ and $h=1$, in which no correlations can be established and the inequality is never violated. Note that violations are more difficult in longer chains, which tend to the noninteracting curve, and that the first violation time is ordered with $n$ (see Fig.~\ref{fig3}).}
\label{LG_spatial}
\end{figure}

Let us now discuss our numerical results for the Hamiltonian \eqref{XXHamiltonian}, presented in Fig.~\ref{LG_spatial} for the values of $J=1$ and $h = 1$.
We first note that setting $J \neq 0$ allows for violations of the inequality, which occur at times close to the maxima of the noninteracting curve that we are modifying by considering $J>0$.
We also observe that the noninteracting profile is increasingly recovered as 
$n$ increases.
%
Fig.~\ref{LG_spatial} also shows that, if there are violations for a certain initial state, Hamiltonian and measurements, then increasing the length of the chain tends to increase the first violation time.
This phenomenon is a consequence of the existence of a maximum velocity, the Lieb-Robinson bound \cite{lieb1972}, at which correlations can propagate in a spin chain. 

Besides this general result, we note that the details of the Hamiltonian and of the measurements matter when analyzing \textit{quantitatively} the strength of the violation and the specific violation times.
Indeed, for a given setup, the measurements may be more or less optimal for observing a violation at a given time.
To reduce this variability, we perform an optimization on the measurement to maximize the value of $K_n$ at each time step \cite{budroni2013bounding}. 
The details of such optimization are discussed in the App.~\ref{appB}, where we indeed verify that the strength of the violation can be substantially increased with respect to the non-optimized case (see Tab~\ref{tab_max_viol}). Moreover, as expected, the violation strength diminishes for long chains and tends to the noninteracting $J=0$ curve, especially for short times. 

To further probe the relation between the spatial LGI violation and the correlation dynamics, we plot in Fig.~\ref{fig3} the optimized first-violation times $\tau$ versus $n$.
The data for Eq.~\eqref{XXHamiltonian} (blue dots) show a linear relation between these quantities. 
Therefore, we verify that the spatial Leggett-Garg inequality enables us to reconstruct the light-cone dynamics with which quantum correlations spread in the chain, and thus provides a lower-bound estimate for the Lieb-Robinson velocity~\cite{lieb1972}. 
To confirm the connection between Lieb-Robinson physics and the inequality violation, we include next nearest-neighbor (NNN) interactions in the Hamiltonian.
In particular, by adding the term $J\sum_{i=1}^{N-2}\vec{\sigma}_i\cdot \vec{\sigma}_{i+2}$ to Eq.~\eqref{XXHamiltonian}, we observe again a linear proportionality between $\tau$ and $n$, but the slope of the curve decreases (orange squares in Fig.~\ref{fig3}).
This shows that the spatial Leggett-Garg inequality violation is accelerated by enabling longer-range interactions that allow for a faster spreading of quantum correlations. 
Note that, for small system sizes, we actually obtain that the inequality is violated for any finite time $t>0$ (see the inset for $n=2,3$). 
This occurs because the sites $1,n,2n-1$ are directly coupled through the extended Hamiltonian for $n=2,3$, while for $n>3$ these sites do not interact directly and a finite time is needed to violate the LGI.  

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=.99\linewidth]{fig3.pdf}
%\captionsetup{footnotes}
\caption{Time of the first noticeable inequality violation, $\tau$ versus distance $n$, showing that the speed of quantum correlations propagation in the spin chain is limited (we take the criteria $K_n>1.02$ to determine $\tau$). The points correspond to nearest-neighbor (NN) Hamiltonian Eq.~\eqref{XXHamiltonian} with $J=1, h = 1$ while the squares to its next-nearest neighbor (NNN) extension. The straight lines fit points $2\leq n\leq 6 $. The inset shows the spatial Leggett-Garg evolution maximized over measurements in normalized time for the NNN case and various distances $n$.  }
\label{fig3}
\end{figure}

The protocol of our work can be implemented in the available experimental platforms 
\cite{browaeys2020many, PRXQuantum.3.020303, geier2021floquet, miller2024two, bloch2012quantum, gross2017, doi:10.1126/science.abk2397, blatt2012quantum, RevModPhys.93.025001, jepsen2020spin, PhysRevX.11.011011}. 
In such experiments, the spin state is imaged at the prescribed times and sites and the outcome of the measurements are recorded. After enough rounds of collecting data, the statistics involved in LGI Eq.~\eqref{LG_spatial} can be inferred. As a final remark, we note that measuring in different sites helps preventing the clumsiness loophole which could otherwise attribute violations of macrorealism to imperfect measurements rather than genuine quantum effects.


In conclusion, we conceptually extend the Leggett-Garg inequality to analyze the correlation dynamics of a quantum many-body system. The inequality violation discriminates between the noninteracting and the interacting regime of a Heisenberg chain in a magnetic field. In the interacting case, we find that the violation time increases with the distance between the measuring parties, showing that quantum correlations require a finite time to establish in spin chains. Such time can be reduced by extending the interaction range from nearest neighbors to next-to-nearest neighbors. Our work can be readily implemented in the existing experimental platforms, allowing to fundamentally test non-relativistic quantum dynamics of many-body systems. 
\\

\noindent\textbf{Codes and data statement}.-- The correlators are computed using exact diagonalization methods (for small $n$) and standard time-evolving block-decimation (TEBD) and time dependent variational principle (TDVP) for the NN and NNN cases respectively implemented in TeNPy. Codes and data to generate the figures can be found in \cite{tobepublished}. 



