\subsection{Forecast Evaluation and Model Selection}

To assess kernel performance, we compare simulated distributions \( \tilde{\mathcal{D}}^{X,m}_\omega \) of forecasted counts against realized counts \( N^{X,Y,r}_{(\tau,\tau+\xi]} \), computing the log-likelihood loss:
\begin{equation}
    \ell^{X,m} = -\sum_{j=1}^T \log \mathbb{P}\left( N^{X,Y,r}_{\omega_j} \mid \tilde{\mathcal{D}}^{X,m}_{\omega_j} \right)
\end{equation}

Model selection is based on the test for superior predictive ability (SPA) \cite{hansen2005test}, which compares competing kernels under common evaluation metrics while controlling for multiple testing.

The best-performing kernel \( \hat{\Phi}^{*} \) is used for all subsequent simulations. The resulting forecasting-based decision rule \( \chi^{\mathcal{H}}(w) \) is benchmarked against the oracle decision \( \chi^m(w) \) using the agreement score defined in Problem~1.2.

