\subsection*{Convention}
Throughout this paper, we use the following convention.
\bnum
\item For a statement $P$, we set $\delta(P)$ to be $1$ or $0$ depending on whether $ P$ is true or not. In particular, we set $\delta_{i,j}=\delta(i =j)$. 

\item For $a\in \Z \cup \{ -\infty \} $ and $b\in \Z \cup \{ \infty \} $ with $a\le b$, we set 
\begin{align*}
& [a,b] =\{  k \in \Z \ | \ a \le k \le b\}, &&  [a,b) =\{  k \in \Z \ | \ a \le k < b\}, \allowdisplaybreaks\\
& (a,b] =\{  k \in \Z \ | \ a < k \le b\}, &&  (a,b) =\{  k \in \Z \ | \ a < k < b\},
\end{align*}
and call them \emph{intervals}. 
When $a> b$, we understand them as empty sets. For simplicity, when $a=b$, we write $[a]$ for $[a,b]$. 

\item For sets $A$ and $J$, we write $A^{J}$ for the product of copies of $A$ indexed by $J$.
We simply write $A^m := A^{[1,m]}$ if no confusion arises.
\ee

