\begin{abstract}
In this work, a field theory model containing a real scalar singlet and an SU(2) symmetry preserving complex doublet is studied using the method of lattice simulations. The model considers all quartic vertices along with the Yukawa vertex between a real scalar singlet and an SU(2) symmetry preserving complex doublet field. Machine learning is used to extract representative functions of the field propagators, lattice regulator, and the Yukawa vertex. In the considered renormalization scheme the field propagators are found enhanced compared to their respective tree level structure. It is found that mixing of operators containing scalar singlet with SU(2) invariant field operators results in $0^{+}$ states with a peculiar scarcity in hundreds of $GeV$s. The Yukawa vertex shows weak dependence on the field momenta while the theory remain interactive as found by the renormalized field propagators. The impact of the real scalar quartic self interaction is found mitigated due to other interaction vertices. The field expectation values exhibit a certain classification despite no conclusive signal of phase transition.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
There are strong reasons to expect physics beyond the Standard Model (BSM) \cite{ATLAS:2023tkl,Cirigliano:2013lpa,Adhikari:2020vqo,Guo:2010hq} which is currently the most widely accepted description of the observed physics in low energy accelerator experiments. Perturbation theory \cite{Schwartz:2013pla,Kaku:1993ym} suffices to describe a significant part of the observed physics. However, there are at least two scenarios which require non-perturbative investigations \cite{Roberts:1994dr,Ruthe:2008rut} for BSM related studies. First, if the new physics involves high couplings, as is the case with QCD interactions \cite{Gattringer:2010gl}, it imposes severe limitation on perturbation theory due to the very construct of the method. Second, if bound states \cite{Maas:2014pba} are to be studied which is the case generally true for the known and yet to be known physics.
\par
Since scalar particles, other than the Higgs boson, are among the actors for the new physics, triviality of $\phi^{4}$ theory \cite{Jora:2015yga} naturally necessitates a thorough understanding of scalar sector as well as its extensions to incorporate interactions with other types of particles. The models including $\phi^{4}$ interactions serve to probe the role of the interaction in a larger theory containing other interactions in different regions of the respective parameter spaces. An encouraging observation is that the models containing the Higgs interactions are not found trivial \cite{Maas:2013aia,Maas:2014pba}. Beside addressing the problem of triviality, such investigations may also be informative of other aspects such as the role of the SM Higgs portal \cite{Lebedev:2021xey,Arcadi:2021mag} to scalar sector and how to differentiate the Higgs interactions with other scalar fields sharing similar symmetries.
\par
Scalar interactions have already been studied for various reasons \cite{Lawrence:2022afv} including studying the phase transition \cite{De:2005ny,Zarikas:1995qb}, dark matter related physics \cite{Kim:2023pwf,Mondino:2020lsc,Ahmed:2022tfm,Ahmed:2022qeh}, and for the Higgs portal \cite{Lebedev:2021xey,Arcadi:2021mag}. However, either smaller models are studied which may pertain to a larger scalar sector, or the study is based on perturbation theory \cite{Boehm:2020wbt} to address the related phenomenology. As presence of other interactions, and the corresponding symmetries, may significantly effect the overall behavior of the model, it begs for detailed study of the sub-theories as well as the larger (parent) theory in different regions of the respective parameter space. Naturally, embarking on such a task requires non-perturbative methods. It is acknowledged that such attempts have been made, see \cite{vandeBruck:2022xbk} for instance, though it addresses a different matter than the aspects to be considered here. Furthermore, the models containing a variety of interactions also serve as a test bed to observe peculiar interactions, such as scalar Yukawa interaction, due to their extraordinary characteristics.
\par
The methods of lattice simulations (LS) and Dyson Schwinger equations (DSEs) are commonly known non-perturbative methods. Both have certain strengths and limitations. For DSEs, there are infinite equations with generally more unknown correlation functions than the considered number of equations. Hence, using ansatz and truncations are not uncommon. For LS, the cutoff is not known a priori which is a necessary quantity to extract other important quantities, such as field propagators. Furthermore, being a Monte Carlo based method it requires considerable computation resources which ultimately restricts the extent of LS based investigations. However, a combination of both of the methods is certainly promising for new physics in which lattice results are used as input to DSEs. Availability of the lattice results for the method of DSEs is indeed a strong merit of such investigations. Machine learning (ML) serves as a highly important tool to extract mathematical structure which suitably represents the considered quantity.
\par
This paper encompasses the study of a field theory containing a real scalar singlet field and an SU(2) preserving complex doublet field termed as the Higgs field for convenience as well as future reference. All renormalizable interactions except the cubic scalar self interaction are included in the model. As opposed to smaller theories, it involves a gigantic parameter space comprised of masses and couplings. Hence, the Higgs (bare) mass and the quartic Higgs couplings were kept constant during the entire study. Such a large model contains certain sub-theories interesting from different perspectives, including phenomenology. Accordingly, the investigation focuses on the features independent of consideration of any particular physics.
\par
The study primarily addresses three aspects in scalar interactions. First, how a trivial sub-theory residing in a larger (parent) theory effects the correlation functions, particularly the field propagators, is ascertained. Second, implications of the Yukawa interaction in the model are considered, particularly by demonstrating the correlation between the coupling strengths and the quantities later described. Third, the role of $0^{+}$ operators in the scalar sector, particularly with respect to the resulting masses, is ascertained. From the numerical perspective, an additional goal is to extract representative functions of the field propagators, Yukawa vertex, and the lattice spacing (regulator) which are useful for DSE related investigations. Supervised machine learning \cite{MarslandML} becomes a natural choice for this purpose.
\par
The current investigation is a part of a larger study of scalar sector aimed at developing thorough understanding of the Higgs portal and the other relevant aspects in the sector. There are 2 sibling papers \cite{tajdar:2025lat1,tajdar:2025lat2} to be shortly reported, which further considers the sector.
