\subsection{Higgs and scalar ball Operators}
\begin{figure} 
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{higgsballs.eps}
 \caption{The Higgs ball operator for $2^{nd}$ smearing level are plotted against the parameters $(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:higgsballs}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} 
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{latspace.eps}
 \caption{Lattice spacing with machine learnt deviations against the parameters $(\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:latspacing}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{sballvshball1.eps}
 \caption{Difference between scalar ball mass and the Higgs ball mass is plotted against the parameters $(\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:sballvshball1}
\end{figure}
As mentioned in section \ref{section:TechnicalDetails}, presence of 3 exponentials renders determination of scale anything but straight forward, see Figure \ref{fig:higgsballs}, and requires robust algorithms for curve fitting. The calculated lattice spacing is observed forming two trajectories against scalar mass for different couplings. How a lattice spacing chooses the trajectory is highly non-linear but the general trend is that the logarithm of lattice spacing reduces as the logarithm of scalar mass $\Lambda m_{s}$ increases, see Figure \ref{fig:latspacing}. Yukawa coupling is found to have extremely mild effect which is understandable because there are other interactions with possible contributions.
\par
The lattice spacing was subject to machine learning, see Figure \ref{fig:latspacing}. The function to be trained is given by
\begin{equation}
f(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)=\sum_{i,j,k,l =0}^{5} c_{ijk} \alpha^{i} \lambda^{j} \gamma^{k} m_{s}^{l}.
\label{eq:latspacfunc1}
\end{equation}
The implementation of SML for the rest of the quantities follows the procedure described in section \ref{section:TechnicalDetails}. Figure \ref{fig:latspacing} shows the presence of strong deviations which is due to a variety of factors including the statistical fluctuations, limitation of the training function, lattice artifacts, and most importantly the non-linearity mentioned above. Hence, functions with stronger non-linear characteristics than in  equation \ref{eq:latspacfunc1} may be required to improve suppression of the respective cost function. The coefficients are included in Appendix A.
\par
Another contender for determination of lattice spacing (regulator) could have been the scalar ball. Despite the previously mentioned argument against the scalar ball, contrasting subtle difference between the two operators is important because the scalar ball operator may have relevance to low energy (restricted cutoff) theories. The Higgs ball and scalar ball operators are juxtaposed in Figure \ref{fig:sballvshball1} in order to probe the subtleties in lattice masses, $m^{opr}_{h}$ and $m^{opr}_{s}$. The mass $m^{opr}_{s}$ is not found to be extremely higher than $m^{opr}_{h}$. However, for higher scalar mass it tends to have considerably lower values compared to the Higgs ball operators' with few exceptions. If not due to lattice artifacts, it indicates existence of a certain structure with sensitivity in the parameter space. It serves as an impediment to perform machine learning as it does for the case of $m^{opr}_{h}$.
