 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{0plus-masses3b.eps}
 \caption{$0^{+}$ masses against the parameters $(\alpha,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:0plusmasses3b}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{0plus-masses4b.eps}
 \caption{$0^{+}$ masses against the parameters $(\alpha,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:0plusmasses4b}
\end{figure}
The richness of the model in terms of $0^{+}$ states is remarkable. The model contains very heavy as well as masses lighter than numerical precision, see Figures \ref{fig:0plusmasses1}-\ref{fig:0plusmasses3} \footnote{It is important to mention here that the included heavy masses can not be taken as unreliable as the numerics were carefully considered to access them. The extraordinary rise of the magnitude is only due to the low values of lattice spacing whose numerical effects were also taken into account.} which are due to the operators $O_{1}(t)$-$O_{9}(t)$ in equations \ref{eq:0p-oper1}. They indicate that, if not a null state (with mass zero), the model does contain masses significantly lighter than the neutrinos. These masses fall into the category of ultra light masses \cite{Hui:2016ltb}. Another observation is relative scarcity of states between $1$ eV and $1$ TeV while the states accumulate in terms of groups. It does encourage further examination of the sector in conjunction with SM, or at least the sub-theories of it.
\par
The effects of scalar singlet can be ascertained by comparing the observation to the results using gauge invariant operators as it is the gauge invariant physics which is observed during low energy collision experiments. Such operators are $O_{0}$, $O_{1}$, and $O_{2}$ defined in equations \ref{eq:0p-oper1}. The $0^{+}$ states from these operators are given in Figures \ref{fig:0plusmasses1b}-\ref{fig:0plusmasses4b}. The repositioning of the states is evident as now there are more states in hundreds of $TeV$s, while the states in $\mu eV$s have been suppressed beyond the available numerical limits. It is reasonable to expect existence of null states among these states. Hence, It is concluded that presence of scalar singlet tends to lift the states of the system.
\par
The observations particularly invites study of two interesting avenues, further exploration from the perspective of low energy effective field theories and inclusion of the \textit{actual} Higgs boson. It may solidify understanding of the low energy physics beside presenting a contrast between the Higgs boson and another SU(2) invariant field.
\subsection{Field Expectations}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{HiggsParameters1.eps}
 \caption{Gauge invariant Higgs parameter is plotted at different strengths of Yukawa coupling $\lambda$ against the parameters $(\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:hpar1}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{ScalarExpectations.eps}
 \caption{Scalar expectation against $\Lambda m_{s}$ in GeVs is plotted at different strengths of Yukawa coupling $\lambda$ against the parameters $(\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:svev}
\end{figure}
Despite the simplicity of one point functions and their expectations, there are certain interesting observations in the model. First, the Higgs one point function vanishes conforming to the custodial symmetry in the model. However, the quantity $<\frac{1}{V_{4d}} \int dt d^{3}x h^{\dagger}h>$, referred to the Higgs parameter, has symmetry of the model which makes it an interesting quantity to study. A peculiar characteristic is classification of the quantity over scalar mass $\Lambda m_{s}$, see Figure \ref{fig:hpar1} which depicts two distinct trajectories. The role of Yukawa coupling, which becomes milder with the decline of its strength, is also evident in the model despite that the corresponding interaction has a different symmetry. It can be taken as an indication that the cubic vertex may not be a null quantity, highlighting its subtle role in the model.
\par
A similar pattern is observed in scalar expectation value, see Figure \ref{fig:svev}, which raises the possibility of a correlation to the above mentioned quantity, except that three trajectories are observed in the model. It is important to note that due to remarkable difference in terms of symmetries, it is not reasonable to expect a similar bifurcation. However, it does indicate existence of a particular structure in the region of strong Yukawa coupling.
\par
The two quantities imply that if there is a region in which only one physics is involved, it may exist at lower values of $\Lambda m_{s}$, particularly at weak Yukawa coupling.
\subsection{Field Propagators}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{sprs1.eps}
 \caption{Scalar propagators (left) and the respective dressing functions (right) for scalar (lattice) mass 10.0 against the parameters $(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:s2p1}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{sprs2.eps}
 \caption{Scalar propagators (left) and the respective dressing functions (right) for scalar (lattice) mass $10^{-6}$ against the parameters $(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:s2p2}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{hprs1.eps}
 \caption{Higgs propagators (left) and the respective dressing functions (right) for scalar (lattice) mass 10.0 against the parameters $(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:h2p1}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure} [h]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = 1.0\textwidth]{hprs2.eps}
 \caption{Higgs propagators (left) and the respective dressing functions (right) for scalar (lattice) mass $10^{-6}$ against the parameters $(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)$.}
 \label{fig:h2p2}
\end{figure}
The field propagators are valuable for our investigation because they carry information regarding triviality, dynamical mass generation, effects due to renormalization scheme, and relevance of perturbation theory. However, a significant impediment to our investigation is presence of large (and diverse) scales (regulators) for not a reasonable size of the lattice. It results in limited access to the infrared region of the model. Hence, application of ML on the propagators becomes relevant in order to extract information from the representative functions while being cautious of effects due to extrapolations. An additive advantage of these functions is reduction of the numerical artifacts in the field propagators. Furthermore, they can be used as input functions to the method of Dyson Schwinger equations with more confidence than higher correlation functions which may have larger statistical fluctuations for the same size of statistics.
\par
For the case of scalar propagators, the function used for training is given by
\par
\begin{equation}\label{eq:spr1}
 f(p,m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)= \frac{1}{p^{2}+m^{2}_{s}+\sum_{i,j,k,l,t =0}^{5} c_{ijklt} \alpha^{i}  (\frac{\lambda}{\Lambda})^{j} \gamma^{k} (\frac{p}{\Lambda})^{l} (\frac{m_{s}}{\Lambda})^{t} }.
\end{equation}
A similar function was used to train for the Higgs propagators, the expression is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hpr1}
 f(p,m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\gamma)= \frac{1}{p^{2}+m^{2}_{h}+\sum_{i,j,k,l,t =0}^{5} c_{ijklt} \alpha^{i} (\frac{\lambda}{\Lambda})^{j} \gamma^{k} (\frac{p}{\Lambda})^{l} (\frac{m_{s}}{\Lambda})^{t} }.
\end{equation}
The effects of regulator, scalar mass, and the quartic scalar self interaction coupling are evident in the field propagators, see Figures \ref{fig:s2p1}-\ref{fig:h2p2}.
\par
First, a general observation is rising of the dressing function significantly above 1, resulting in considerable rise of propagators, which is either due to a possible pole in these Euclidean propagators or a sign of a significantly heavy particle. However, no conclusive evidence of zero crossing is observed throughout the parameter space. It is also found that if bare mass, i.e. $\Lambda m_{s}$ ($\Lambda m_{h}$) for the case of a scalar (Higgs) propagator is used in the renormalization conditions, the propagators exhibits a monotonical decline with rise of momentum once the deep infrared region is crossed. However, the cutoff values are found over a wide range which means suppression of the field propagators even up to the numerical precision, and the bare mass raises to extremely large values due to the cutoff. In order to avoid a likely tension between numerical precision and the precision, which may compromise robustness of ML, the mentioned renormalization scheme was adopted. One may also use a constant value for the bare mass which, in principle, can have any suitable value. For the Higgs propagators, in contrast to scalar propagators, the lattice value of Higgs mass was chosen and kept fixed.
\par
The effects of the chosen parameters appear only in infrared region. Hence, it is the region where machine learning plays significant role in suppressing numerical (lattice) artifacts in the propagators. The structure of the representative functions is chosen to increase the robustness of the algorithm as well as to capture as much physics as possible. In the ultraviolet region the propagators behave as physical particles, and are close to the tree level structure which favors the corresponding representative functions in terms of robustness and reliability for further studies. However, deviations of ML results from LS propagators are evident in infrared region, see Figures \ref{fig:s2p1}-\ref{fig:h2p2}. It is reasonable to conclude that the observed deviations stem from numerical (lattice) artifacts with a likely small contribution from the limitations of the representative functions \ref{eq:spr1} and \ref{eq:hpr1} to entirely capture the details.
\par
The variety of scalar propagators in the parameter space is a strong sign of absence of triviality. It is also verified by other renormalization schemes mentioned earlier in section \ref{section:TechnicalDetails}. There is indeed a significant role of scalar quartic interaction in the model but the model remain interacting, see Figures \ref{fig:s2p1}-\ref{fig:s2p2}. The Higgs propagator also points towards the same conclusion. For higher scalar mass the propagators show strong deviations in infrared region. As the magnitude of scalar mass is lowered, sensitivity of the propagators increases, see Figures \ref{fig:h2p1}-\ref{fig:h2p2}. In any case, the deviations from tree level are evident which indicates that the theory remain interacting.
\par
The phenomenon of dynamical mass generation is non-perturbative in nature and is mostly relevant to fermionic interactions \cite{Curtis:1992gm,Chang:2024xjd,Berbig:2024uwm,ptz061}. However, there is a possible relevance to the investigation \footnote{The phenomenon has also been studied from different perspectives, see \cite{Kan:2023lah} for example.} under the definition that dynamical mass generation takes place when the tree level mass is smaller than the renormalized mass. Figure \ref{fig:s2p2}, which shows scalar propagators of least (in our investigation) bare magnitude, indicates stronger deviations from the tree level in comparison to the Figure \ref{fig:s2p1}. In other renormalization schemes, mentioned earlier, the effect is qualitatively similar which indicates strong deviations from the respective tree level structure in the deep infrared region, hence favoring the renormalized mass greater than the tree level mass. However, how much renormalized mass is produced may be sensitive to the chosen renormalization scheme.
