The scalar Yukawa vertex is found one of the noisiest quantities. Since the model is also observed to have considerable finite volume effects, only the largest feasible lattice size is chosen for all quantities including the vertex. \footnote{There is a sibling paper to be reported with considers the theory, including the vertex, from a wider perspective while also addressing the numerical artifacts in the model.}. The fluctuations emphasize importance of ML for the quantity in order to extract meaningful information. Observing unmistakable role of the Yukawa interaction term in the model suggests that the vertex may not be a null function, despite that it is the only interaction with a different symmetry ($\phi \rightarrow -\phi$). Furthermore, presence of large cutoff values strongly hinders in accessing infrared region unless the lattice size is exceptionally large, which is not the case here.
\par
The following function was used for ML application.
\begin{equation}
F_{\Gamma}(m_{s},\alpha,\lambda,\beta,u,v)=  \lambda [ 1 + \sum_{i,j,k,l,m,n =0}^{i,j,k,l,m,n =4} c_{ijklmn} \tilde{m}_{s}^{i} \alpha^{j} \tilde{\lambda}^{k} \beta^{l} u^{m} v^{n} ]
\end{equation}
where $u=a\underrightarrow{p}.a\underrightarrow{q}$, $v=a\underrightarrow{q}.a\underrightarrow{q}$, $\tilde{m}_{s}=a m_{s}$, and $\tilde{\lambda}_{s}=a \lambda_{s}$, where $a$ is the lattice spacing at the chosen point of the parameter space. $\underrightarrow{p}$ and $\underrightarrow{q}$ are the momentum vectors (in 4 dimensional Euclidean spacetime) of scalar and the Higgs fields, respectively. The vertex function is obtained under the renormalization condition $\Gamma(u(p,q),v(p,q))|_{p=\Lambda,q=\Lambda}$. It is important to mention here that the vertex was not projected on the expansion terms arising from perturbative expansion in the theory. Since the scalar Yukawa coupling is assumed to take any value, such expansions are not as meaningful as in perturbation theory. Hence, the variables $u$ and $v$ were deliberately chosen to expand the vertex in terms of scalar parameters formed by the field momenta respecting the conservation law.
\par
From the LS, the vertex is found weakly depending upon the field momenta over a considerable range of momentum values, see Figures \ref{fig:vertex1}-\ref{fig:vertex4}. However, the trained vertex function captures certain dependence, as shown in the figures, which develops in the region of higher field momentum. The qualitative behavior is found persistent throughout the explored parameter space. It suggests that the vertex may not have appreciable correlation with the classification observed in scalar expectations or the Higgs parameter. If there is a phase structure in the parameter space, the vertex was not found to be sensitive to it.
\section{Conclusion}
The model containing two scalar fields with trivial and SU(2) symmetries is found highly informative from various perspectives. Most importantly, the study serves as a demonstration of how effects of a trivial sub-theory are mitigated once it is extended by inclusion of another scalar field, a complex Higgs doublet in this case. The field propagators are informative in this regard as their dressing functions effectively probe the feature in the model. The model demonstrates how gauge invariant $0^{+}$ states are effected once a scalar singlet field is introduced. Furthermore, a certain dependence in the parameter space is also found which requires further probing, in particular to ascertain the precise role of the Yukawa coupling in the model.
\par
The scalar Yukawa coupling, despite its relatively distinct symmetry, does have a role in the parameter space of the model. It does not vanish which support this conclusion. Furthermore, the action is not found with a wrong sign which points towards the numerical stability of the model containing the vertex.
\par
The $0^{+}$ states in the model are highly diverse in the model, ranging from far above TeV scale to significantly below the eV scale. In particular, if there are (ultra) light scalars in nature, the model becomes a highly attractive avenue for a comprehensive study. Furthermore, the scarcity of states around $100$ GeV supports its position as an attractive candidate for phenomenology.
\par
However, no conclusive indication of phase structure in the explored region of the parameter space is observed. The expectation of the Higgs field (squared) magnitude has a peculiar behavior in the parameter space which qualitatively depicts the same behavior as the scalar expectation does. However, its implications in the relevant particle physics phenomenology are yet to be understood.
\par
It is concluded that the model serves as a highly non-trivial platform for further investigations from various perspectives including low energy phenomenology, further extensions as well as sub-models, and theoretical understanding the of the sector.
