\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we first study the large deviation principle (LDP) for non-degenerate McKean-Vlasov stochastic differential equations (MVSDEs)   with   H\"{o}lder continuous drifts by using Zvonkin's transformation. When the drift only satisfies H\"{o}lder condition, the skeleton equation may have  multiple solutions.   Among these solutions, we  find one    that ensures the MVSDEs satisfy the LDP. Moreover, we  introduce a new definition for the rate  function that reduces to traditional rate function if the drift satisfies the Lipschitz condition. Secondly,    we  study the LDP for degenerate MVSDEs with   H\"{o}lder continuous drifts.
\end{abstract}\noindent

AMS Subject Classification: \quad 60H10; \quad 60F10.
\noindent

Keywords: H\"{o}lder continuous; rate function; large deviation principle; MVSDEs.

%\vskip 2cm
\section{Introduction}
Throughout this paper,   let     $(\Omega, \sF, \mF:=\{\sF_{t}\}_{t\geq 0}, P)$ be  a complete probability space and $\{B_t, t\in [0,T]\}$ be a standard  $m$-Brownian motion defined on  this  probability space.
As is well known, LDP  for stochastic differential equations (SDEs) is an important branch of probability theory that provides exponential estimates for probabilities of rare events occurring in the stochastic systems, and it has a wide range of applications such as  in  mathematic finance, statistic mechanics, biology and so on. Furthermore, the LDP  for SDEs  has been studied extensively, see, e.g., \cite{BY, L,ML, MZ, RW} and references therein.

 Recently,  MVSDEs have received much attention. The form of the equation is as the following:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
  &X_t= x_{0}+\int^{t}_{0}b(s, X_s, \mu_{s})\dif s +\int^{t}_{0}\sigma(s,   X_s,  \mu_{s})\dif  B_s, t\in [0, T],\\
 &\mu_{t}=\sL(X_t):= \mbox{the probability distribution of } X_t.
 \end{cases}
 \end{equation*}
Obviously, the coefficients involved depend not only on  the state process but also on its distribution. With contrast to the classical SDEs, MVSDEs enjoy some essential features. The work on MVSDEs was initiated by McKean \cite {M2}, who was inspired by Kac's Programme in Kinetic Theory \cite{K}. Sznitman \cite{S} investigated the existence and uniqueness of the results under the  global Lipschitz condition.  Wang \cite{W} studied the existence of invariant probability measures for MVSDEs.    Raynal \cite{Ra}  analyzed   MVSDEs with the H\"{o}lder drift coefficients.  Furthermore, in addition to the theoretical values, this kind of equations also has a lot of applied values in social science, economics, engineering, etc. (see  e.g. \cite{CD}).
Subsequently, a general LDP for MVSDEs is  established by Dos Reis et al \cite{Dos}, for quasilinear stochastic evolution equations
by Hong et al \cite{RWZ}, for MVSDEs with jumps by Liu et al \cite{RW1},  for multi-scale McKean-Vlasov SDEs by
 by Hong et al \cite{HLLS}, to name a few.

Recently,  Cheng and Huang \cite{CH}  investigates large deviation principle for SDEs with Dini continuous drifts. In their work, the  drift term, being   Dini continuous,  will  vanish when $\epsilon\rightarrow 0.$ However, in  our setting , the H\"{o}lder continuous drift coefficients do not tend to zero as $\epsilon\rightarrow 0$, which leads to essential differences between our work  and  that of \cite{CH}.  For further related work, we refer to   \cite{GY,HJS,RW1, SY} and references  therein.


 Motivated by the above  the literature, we will study the LDP for MVSDEs with H\"{o}lder continuous drift, the  main contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$]  The LDP for non-degenerate MVSDEs with H\"{o}lder continuous conditions is studied by using Zvonkin's transformation. When the drift is H\"{o}lder continuous, the skeleton equation may have  multiple solutions.  Among these solutions, we will find one    that ensures the MVSDEs satisfy the LDP.  Moreover,  we  introduce  a new definition for the rate  function that reduces to traditional rate function if the drift satisfies the Lipschitz condition.

\item[$\bullet$]   
The   LDP for degenerate MVSDEs with the H\"{o}lder continuous drifts is also investigated.
\end{itemize}




We close this part by giving our organization  in this article. In Section 2, we give the preliminaries and main results.  In Section 3, we prove the main results.

 Finally, we make the following convention:
 The letter $C(\eta)$  will denote  positive constant which only depends on $\eta$, whose value may
vary from one place to another.  Additionally, $C$  denotes positive constant and its value may vary in different cases.


