Choosing a sequence $\{\epsilon_{n}\}$ such that $\epsilon_{n}\rightarrow 0$ as $ n\rightarrow \infty,$  one can see  that $\{X^{\epsilon_{n}}\}_{n\geq1}$ is a Cauchy sequence in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}),$ i.e.
\bl\label{202}
Assume $(\mathrm{H2}).$  Then $\{X^{\epsilon_{n}}\}_{n\geq1}$ is a Cauchy sequence in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$
\el

\begin{proof}
By Lemma \ref{201}, Gronwall's inequality and letting $m\rightarrow\infty, n\rightarrow \infty,$  one has
$$\lim_{m,n\rightarrow \infty}\mE\sup_{0\leq t \leq T}|X^{\epsilon_{n}}_{t}-X^{\epsilon_{m}}_{t}|^{2}=0. $$
This implies $\{X^{\epsilon_{n}}\}_{n\geq1 }$ is a Cauchy sequence in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$
\end{proof}
Let $X^{0}$ be the limit of $\{X^{\epsilon_{n}}\}_{n\geq1 }$ in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$ Noting that $b(t,x, \mu)$ is continuous and bounded in both $x$ and $\mu$ from assumption (H2), and letting $\epsilon \rightarrow 0$ in \eqref{a1},
we then have:
\bl\label{12345}
$\{X^{0}_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq T}$ is the solution of Eq.\eqref{44+-}.
\el

\br
The limit $\{X^{0}_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq T}$  is independent of the choice of $\{\epsilon_{n}\}_{n\geq1}.$   In fact,   let $\{\tilde{\epsilon}_{n}\}$ be another sequence such that $\tilde{\epsilon}_{n}\rightarrow 0$ as $n\rightarrow \infty.$  By lemma \ref{202},  we have that  $\{X^{\tilde{\epsilon}_{n}}\}_{n\geq1}$ is  a Cauchy sequence in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$ Denote   the limit of $\{X^{\tilde{\epsilon}_{n}}\}_{n\geq1 }$ in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d})$ by $\tilde{X}^{0}$.   Next,  we  set $\alpha_{1}=\epsilon_{1}, \alpha_{2}=\tilde{\epsilon}_{1}, \alpha_{3}=\epsilon_{2}, \alpha_{4}=\tilde{\epsilon}_{2}, \cdots, \alpha_{2k-1}=\epsilon_{k}, \alpha_{2k}=\tilde{\epsilon}_{k}, \cdots.$    By lemma \ref{202},  one can see that  $\{X^{\alpha_{n}}\}_{n\geq1}$ is  a Cauchy sequence in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$ Let $\bar{X}^{0}$ be the limit of $\{X^{\alpha_{n}}\}_{n\geq1 }$ in $S^{2}(0,T; \mR^{d}).$   Then $X^{0}_{t}=\bar{X}_{t}=\tilde{X}^{0}_{t}, t\in [0, T].$


\er





In the following, we aim to illustrate that $\{X^{0}_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq T}$ ensures $\{X^{\epsilon}_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq T}$  satisfies the LDP. Recall that  $\mu_{t}$ is the distribution of $X_t$ and let $\mu_{t}^{\epsilon}$ be the distribution of $X_t^\epsilon$. Write
 $b^{\mu}_{t}(x):=b(t,x,\mu_{t}), b^{\mu^\epsilon}_{t}(x):=b(t,x,\mu_{t}^\epsilon), \sigma_{t}(x):=\sigma(t,x).$
