\begin{abstract}
%The local magnetic moments of the atoms can be designated by different colors. Two-color, or black-and white groups, or the so-called Shubnikov groups have been used in crystallography to classify different magnets. While applications of multicolor groups other than two-color groups to classify solids have seldom been reported. Here we develop a multicolor group classification scheme to classify all kinds of solids via the symmetry group of their lattices, including nonmagnetic materials and magnets with collinear or non-collinear magnetism, with or without spin-orbit couplings. The advantages of this scheme is that we can unify the classifications into a single framework through multicolor group theory. Especially, non-magnetic compounds fall into 0-color groups, ferro/ferrimagnets fall into 1-color groups, antiferromagnets, as usual, fall into 2-color groups, and non-collinear magnets fall into the multicolor groups descriptions. Moreover, confusions brought about by conventional magnetic-group classification can be removed via this new scheme, a small price to pay is less mathematical elegance for the partitions.

Just like we classify lions and tigers into the same panthera, but into different species, we can classify molecules and solids by their spatial and spin space symmetries. In magnetic molecules and solids, local moments of the atoms can be represented by different colors; we can therefore classify these materials by playing with colors. The advantage of the multicolor group scheme is that it unifies the classifications into a single framework through multicolor group theory, including the recently identified altermagnets and $p$-wave magnets. In particular, non-magnetic compounds fall into 0-color groups, ferro/ferrimagnets fall into 1-color groups, antiferromagnets, as usual, fall into 2-color groups, and non-collinear magnets fall into the simple and complex multicolor groups. Moreover, confusions brought about by conventional magnetic-group classification can be resolved through this new framework.
%, with the small price of sacrificing some mathematical elegance in the partitions. 
\end{abstract}

