\section{Conclusion}

A new method for three-dimensional millimeter-wave imaging was presented and experimentally demonstrated. By combining active incoherent millimeter-wave imaging with traditional radar pulse compression, sufficient spatial and temporal incoherence can be obtained to create cross-range images in azimuth and elevation using interferometric Fourier imaging approaches while simultaneously illuminating the scene with a known waveform that can be processed via pulse compression for high resolution down-range processing. We demonstrated the technique in a 38~GHz active incoherent millimeter-wave imaging system, showing that the approach successfully tracks a single reflecting target and multiple targets simultaneously at different angles and ranges. The proof-of-concept results in this paper show the feasibility of the technique, and point to areas for further improvement, such as increased range and mitigating the impacts of range sidelobes. The demonstrated method may prove useful in a range of imaging applications, including security, home health, remote sensing, and human-computer interaction.

%In this work, we presented a new approach for three-dimensional millimeter-wave imaging by combining active incoherent imaging with pulse compression. By transmitting a known pulsed waveform like an LFM signal alongside incoherent noise signals. This allows the preservation of Fourier-domain interferometric imaging for cross-range (azimuth and elevation), while also extracting down-range information through matched filtering of the known pulse. This combination overcomes a limitation of traditional Fourier-domain imaging systems, which typically cannot provide range. We demonstrated the approach using a 38~GHz system, showing that we can reconstruct both azimuth-elevation images and azimuth-range slices depending on how the data is processed. Together, these measurements provide full 3D information about the scene.%In this article we introduced a new method of 3D millimeter-wave imaging based on the combination of active incoherent millimeter-wave imaging and pulse compression by implementing a LFM signal transmission using known pulsed waveforms. The approach overcomes the traditional limitation of Fourier domain imagers where range information cannot easily be obtained. Experiments using a 38~GHz imaging system demonstrated both Fourier domain cross-range imaging in the azimuth and elevation domains, along with down-range and azimuth cross-range imaging using pulse compression. Combined, these measurements yield a 3D image of the scene.






