\section{Experimental evaluation}\label{Sec.ExperimentalEvaluation}

This section provides a comprehensive analysis of FedFog’s performance under various FL scenarios, comparing it against key baselines such as FogFaaS, Vanilla FL, and Random Client Selection. The evaluation spans multiple dimensions including latency, energy consumption, model accuracy, cold start behavior, and scalability. By simulating diverse client heterogeneity, data drift, and varying hyperparameters, the experiments demonstrate that FedFog consistently delivers superior trade-offs between accuracy, efficiency, and robustness. The inclusion of stress tests, adversarial scenarios, and differential privacy simulations further reinforces FedFog’s practical relevance in real-world edge environments. The results validate FedFog as an adaptive and resource-aware FL orchestration framework.















%\subsection{Benchmark-Based Parameter Calibration} To ensure that FedFog realistically simulates edge--fog environments, we calibrated core system parameters based on empirical observations from real-world platforms such as OpenFaaS and Flower. Cold start delays were modeled using a uniform distribution $\delta_{\text{cold}} \sim U(300, 800)$ ms, which aligns with measurements taken on Raspberry Pi 4 devices running OpenFaaS \cite{openfaas-coldstart}, where container initialization commonly ranges between 350--700 ms depending on image size. Warm start delays were configured between 50--150 ms, corresponding to cached container re-use observed in Kubernetes environments.  Network latency between edge and fog layers (5--15 ms) and fog to cloud nodes (50--100 ms) was derived from LAN benchmarks and public cloud latency reports, closely matching round-trip times in typical smart city and IoT deployments.  Data drift was simulated using KL-divergence metrics and periodic class distribution shifts across edge nodes. These patterns were inspired by real-world temporal drift seen in mobile sensor datasets such as HAR and reflect non-stationary user behavior in edge systems.  As an initial validation step, we benchmarked a Flower-based FL setup using OpenFaaS on local edge nodes and compared it against FedFog’s simulation outputs. Results showed that average per-round latency deviated by only 6--12\% from the real deployment, confirming the effectiveness of FedFog in approximating performance under constrained edge conditions.  Further validation with additional real-world benchmarks is proposed as future work to enhance confidence in simulation fidelity.



%subsection{Metrics}To comprehensively assess the performance of the proposed FedFog framework, we employed four key evaluation metrics that reflect both learning effectiveness and system efficiency. First, global \textbf{model accuracy} was tracked after each federated training round to quantify the learning performance over time. This metric served as the primary indicator of convergence and model generalization across non-IID client data. Second, we measured average round-trip \textbf{latency}, which includes the cumulative time required for local training on clients, serverless function invocation, and global aggregation. This metric highlights the real-time responsiveness of the federated learning workflow under serverless execution constraints.Third, \textbf{energy consumption} was monitored across all participating edge nodes throughout the simulation. Each device's energy usage was computed based on CPU utilization, memory access, and communication overhead, enabling a system-wide analysis of energy efficiency. Finally, we recorded  \textbf{cold start frequency} where serverless functions were invoked without a warm container—on a per-round basis. High cold start frequency reflects inefficiencies in resource orchestration and directly impacts latency and energy. Together, these metrics provide a balanced view of accuracy, responsiveness, sustainability, and orchestration quality within the FedFog simulation.

\subsection{Simulation Environment}

The simulation environment is built on top of the iFogSim toolkit \cite{gupta2017ifogsim}, extended with FedFog-specific modules to support serverless FL. The simulated infrastructure consists of heterogeneous edge nodes—emulating smart wearables, cameras, and IoT sensors—connected to fog gateways and micro data centers. Each edge node receives a private, non-IID data partition, reflecting realistic privacy-preserving setups and heterogeneous user behaviors. Devices are further modeled with varying compute capacities, energy constraints, and cold start delays to mirror real-world deployment conditions. Cold starts are emulated by injecting randomized latency during the first function call, capturing the overheads typical of platforms like OpenFaaS \cite{ghaseminya2025fogfaas}.

FedFog was evaluated under two representative edge AI scenarios. The first, \textit{Smart Healthcare}, leverages the Human Activity Recognition (HAR) dataset \cite{anguita2013public}, where each edge node simulates a mobile user generating multivariate sensor signals (See Figure~\ref{fig:evaluation_protocol}). This setup captures device-level variability and mobility. The second, \textit{Edge Vision}, uses the EMNIST dataset \cite{cohen2017emnist} to model character recognition across distributed visual input sources. By assigning subsets of characters to specific devices, we simulate personalized user behaviors in non-IID settings such as handwriting or keyboard interfaces~\cite{9984832}.
