\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
The parameter $\fNL$, defined as the amplitude of the second-order corrections in the local model of inflationary non-Gaussianity, plays a fundamental role in modern cosmology by quantifying deviations from Gaussianity in the primordial density fluctuations that seeded cosmic structure. A precise measurement of $\fNL$ allows us to test beyond the simplest single-field inflationary scenarios, where fluctuations are nearly Gaussian and fully described by the two-point correlation function, against more complex models (e.g. multi-field dynamics or non-standard kinetic terms) that naturally generate non-Gaussian signatures (see, e.g., \cite{baas,planck2015b}). These primordial non‑Gaussian imprints are most directly probed via the temperature anisotropies of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) radiation, which deliver a high‑precision map of fluctuations at the surface of last scattering.\\
The Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) radiation, observed as small temperature anisotropies on the sky, provides a precise provides of the primordial density fluctuations at the surface of last scattering. Therefore, to analyze these anisotropies and infer non-Gaussian signatures, we model the observed temperature field $T(x)$ on the sphere $\mathbb{S}^2$ as a realization of an isotropic spherical random field, decomposed in spherical harmonics:

$$
T(x) = \sum_{\ell\geq 1}\sum_{m=-\ell}^\ell a_{\ell m}Y_{\ell, m}(x),
$$
where the $Y_{\ell ,m}$ describe an orthonormal basis on the sphere $\mathbb{S}^2$ and the corresponding random harmonic coefficients $a_{\ell, m}$ contain the field's statistical properties (see for example \cite{MaPeCUP}). The assumption of isotropy, that is, statistical invariance under rotations, is well motivated by the cosmological principle and greatly simplifies inference by decoupling different harmonic modes.
If we additionally assume Gaussianity, motivated by the fact that simplest single-field inflationary models predict nearly Gaussian fluctuations, we establish a clear baseline where the field $T_G$ is defined by 
\[
T_G=\sum_{\ell\geq1}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell} a_{\ell,m;G}Y_{\ell,m}(x),
\]
where the Gaussian harmonic coefficients \(\{a_{\ell, m;G}\}\) satisfy  $\mathbb{E}\left[ a_{\ell,m;G} \bar a_{\ell^\prime,m^\prime;G}\right]=C_{\ell;G}\delta_{\ell}^{\ell^\prime}\delta_{m}^{m^\prime}$. The angular power spectrum $\lbrace C_{\ell ;G}\rbrace$ encodes all the stochastic information of $T_G$, while its covariance function depends only on the angular separation
\[
\mathbb{E}\left[T_G(x)\bar{T}_G(y)\right] = \sum_{\ell\geq 1} \frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell;G} P_\ell(\langle x,y\rangle).
\]
Under these assumptions, all odd-order moments of the harmonic coefficients vanish. 
Hence, the full spectral bispectrum, which captures third‑order correlations in harmonic space, defined by
$$
B_{\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3}^{m_1,m_2,m_3}
=\mathbb{E}\left[a_{\ell_1m_1}\,a_{\ell_2m_2}\,a_{\ell_3m_3}\right],
$$
vanishes under the assumption of a Gaussian, isotropic field (see e.g. \cite{dp19,planck2015c}).\\
Realistic inflationary physics often induces small but measurable fluctuations from  Gaussianity and isotropy. Introducing a perturbed field of the form
$$
T(x) = T_G(x) + \fNL\left(T_G^2(x)-\mathbb{E}\left[T_G(x)\right]^2\right),
$$
where $T_G$ is a mean-zero Gaussian isotropic field, provides a minimal, theoretically motivated framework to capture these non-Gaussian features (cf. \cite{marpec4,planck2015b}). Such perturbations are necessary both to reflect the imprint of interactions during inflation and to enable observational searches for new physics beyond the simplest models.
To extract a rotation-invariant signature of third-order correlations, one compresses the full bispectrum via the so-called Wigner-3j symbols into the reduced bispectrum, given by
$$
B_{\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3} = \sum_{m_1,m_2,m_3}
\begin{pmatrix}
	\ell_1 & \ell_2 & \ell_3\\
	m_1 & m_2 & m_3
\end{pmatrix}
\mathbb{E}\left[a_{\ell_1,m_1}\,a_{\ell_2,m_2}\,a_{\ell_3,m_3}\right].
$$
Under the local non-Gaussian model, this reduced bispectrum is proportional to $\fNL$.
The Komatsu–Spergel–Wandelt (KSW) estimator, see for example \cite{ksw05,Mun14,planck2015b}, exploits this fact to construct an optimal, unbiased estimate of $\fNL$ from observed maps. Denoting by \[
\widehat{B}_{\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3}=\sum_{m_1,m_2,m_3}
\begin{pmatrix}
	\ell_1 & \ell_2 & \ell_3\\
	m_1 & m_2 & m_3
\end{pmatrix}a_{\ell_1,m_1}\,a_{\ell_2,m_2}\,a_{\ell_3,m_3}
\] the observed reduced bispectrum, the estimator reads
$$
\fNLest
=\frac
{\displaystyle\sum_{rL\leq \ell_1<\ell_2<\ell_3 \leq L} 
	B_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}\widehat{B}_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}\,
	C_{\ell_1;G}^{-1}C_{\ell_2;G}^{-1}C_{\ell_3;G}^{-1}}
{\displaystyle\sum_{rL\leq \ell_1<\ell_2<\ell_3 \leq L}
	\left(B_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}\right)^2\,
	C_{\ell_1;G}^{-1}C_{\ell_2;G}^{-1}C_{\ell_3;G}^{-1}}.
$$
Here, $L$ denotes the maximum multipole considered, which is determined by the experimental resolution or computational constraints, to ensure the estimator remains tractable in practice, while  $r\in (0,1/2)$ encodes a narrow-band strategy, commonly adopted to reduce the estimator's sensitivity to low-frequency multipoles. This choice guarantees that the number of terms retained in the numerator is still asymptotically large (i.e., of order $L^3$), while effectively focusing the estimation on the high-frequency content of the field. In this paper, we develop a refined asymptotic analysis, valid in the limit as the maximum multipole cutoff $L$ grows to infinity, of this KSW-type estimator in the high-frequency regime. %Building on recent results in spherical harmonic analysis and probabilistic limit theory, we establish exact variance formulas for the estimator under weak non-Gaussian perturbations to  derive central limit theorems characterizing its convergence rate and finite-sample corrections.
%Note that more complex interactions and correlations can be also studied by using higher-order terms such as the trispectrum. According for example to \cite{} Studying the trispectrum is particularly interesting as it provides complementary information about the dynamics of inflation, including possible contributions from higher-derivative interactions, multiple fields, or non-trivial initial states.
More in detail, we establish a quantitative central limit theorem (CLT) for the KSW estimator of the primordial non-Gaussianity parameter $\fNL$ by employing fourth-moment central limit theorem techniques, by \cite{noupebook}, stating that a sequence of random variables living in a given Wiener chaos is characterized by a central limit theorem  if and only if the sequence of their fourth cumulants converges to zero, providing also a rate of convergence. In particular, we prove
\[
	\dW\left( \fNLest - \fNL, Z \right) \leq 
	\tilde C L^{-2},
\]
where $\dW$ is the total variance distance, and $\tilde C>0$ is a positive constant to be discussed below.
 This quantitative approach not only solidifies the theoretical underpinnings of the KSW estimator but also ensures its robustness for analyzing CMB data, offering a rigorous foundation for interpreting $\fNL$ constraints in both current and future cosmological surveys.\\
  An intuitive way to see the \(L^{-2}\) rate is to note that the number of admissible multipole triplets grows like \(N_L=O(L^3)\). Hence \(L^{-2}=O(N_L^{-2/3})\), which outpaces the usual \(O(N_L^{-1/2})\) decay of a standard CLT. This super‑parametric exponent  reflects the cubic volume growth of permissible modes combined with the boundary‑dominated error in approximating sums by integrals, much like the classical Gauss circle problem where lattice-point counting yields a boundary-dominated error.  Similar behaviors appear across discrepancy theory, lattice-point enumeration, and empirical process theory (see, e.g., \cite{niederreiter}).

The structure of the paper is as follows. In Section \ref{sec:preliminary}, we present key results on spherical Gaussian and perturbed random fields, along with a discussion of both theoretical and sample bispectra. Section \ref{sec:OLS} is devoted to the construction and motivation of the estimator for $\fNL$. In Section \ref{sec:main}, we state our main result, a quantitative central limit theorem for the estimator of $\fNL$, and collect the pivotal auxiliary results necessary for its proof. Finally, Section \ref{sec:proofs} collects all the proofs.

\section{Preliminary results}\label{sec:preliminary}
This section collects some results concerning harmonic analysis on the sphere and the construction of non-Gaussian random fields over the sphere when they can be considered as perturbed Gaussian ones. We begin by examining the harmonic expansion of these fields with an emphasis on their stochastic properties. Next, we revisit the angular bispectrum, which quantifies the three-point correlation function of spherical harmonics and serves as a crucial measure of non-Gaussianity, effectively capturing deviations from purely Gaussian behavior in the underlying field. For further details and discussions, the reader is referred, for example, to \cite{MaPeCUP, steinweiss,yadrenko} and the references therein.
\subsection{Gaussian random fields and their stochastic properties}
Let us start by introducing some notation. From now on, $x =\left(\vartheta,\varphi\right) \in \sphere$ denotes the generic spherical coordinate. Also, let $\diff x=\sin\vartheta \diff\vartheta \diff\phi$ be the spherical Lebesgue measure, while $\Ltwo=L^2\left(\mathbb{S}^2,\diff x \right)$ is the space of square-integrable functions over the sphere with respect to the measure $\diff x$. As well-known in the literature (see, for example, \cite {MaPeCUP,steinweiss,vilenkin}), the set of spherical harmonics $\left\lbrace\Y :\ell \geq 0; m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell \right\rbrace$ provides an orthonormal basis for $\Ltwo$. The spherical harmonic can be expressed as the normalized product of the associated Legendre function $\Plm : \left[-1,1\right]\mapsto \mathbb{R}$ of degree $\ell$ and order $m$, and a complex exponential function. Following for example \cite{szego}, the representation is given as as follows
\begin{equation*}
\Y \left(x\right)= 	\Y \left(\vartheta,\varphi\right)=\sqrt{\frac{\lcard}{4\pi} \frac{\left(\ell-m\right)!}{\left(\ell+m\right)!}}\Plm \left(\cos \vartheta \right) e^{i m \varphi},
\end{equation*}
where 
\begin{equation*}
\Plm\left(u\right)=\frac{1}{2^{\ell} \ell !}\left(1-u^2\right)^{\frac{m}{2}}\frac{\diff ^{\ell+m}}{\diff  u^{\ell+m}} \left(u^2-1\right)^{\ell+m}, \quad u \in \left[-1,1\right].
 \end{equation*}
Let $\TG\left( x\right) $ denote a centered, isotropic, mean square continuous, Gaussian random field over the sphere $\sphere$. A random field $\TG$ is isotropic if it is invariant in distribution with
respect to the so-called group of rotations $SO\left(3\right)$,
\begin{align*}
	\TG\left(R x\right) \overset{d}{=} \TG\left(x\right),
\end{align*}
where $R \in SO\left(3\right)$ and $\overset{d}{=}$ denotes equality in distribution. 
The harmonic expansion of $\TG$ is given by%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:TG}
\TG\left( x\right) =\suml \summ \almG \Y \left( x\right) , \quad x\in \sphere,
\end{equation}%
where the term corresponding to $\ell=0$ vanishes due to the centering of $T_G$.
The set of harmonic coefficients $\left\lbrace\almG:\ell \geq 1, m=-\ell ,\dots,\ell\right\rbrace$ contains all the stochastic information related to $T_G$ and are computed by 
\begin{equation*}
\almG=\langle \Y, \TG\rangle_{\sphere}=\int_{\mathbb{S}^{2}}\TG\left( x\right) \Yconj \left( x\right) \diff x,
\end{equation*}
where for any $z \in \mathbb{C}$, $\cc{z}$ denotes its complex conjugate.
Also, as a consequence of the centering of  $\TG$, it directly follows that for any $\ell \geq 1$, $m=-\ell, \ldots, \ell$
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{E}[\almG] = 0.
\end{equation*}
Also, since $Y_{00}(x)=\left(4\pi\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}$ for any $x \in \sphere$,  it follows that $a_{00}=0$ for centered random fields, so that Equation \eqref{eq:TG} reduces to
\begin{equation*}
	\TG\left( x\right) =\sum_{\ell \geq 1} \summ \almG \Y \left( x\right) , \quad x\in \sphere.
\end{equation*}%
In view of the isotropy, for any \(x, y \in \mathbb{S}^2\), the covariance function of \(\TG\) is given by
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{E}[\TG(x)\overline{\TG}(y)] = \suml \frac{2\ell + 1}{4\pi} \ClG P_\ell\left(\langle x, y \rangle\right),
\end{equation*}
where $P_\ell:\left[ -1,1\right] \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ is the Legendre polynomial of degree $\ell$, $\left\lbrace \ClG: \ell \geq 0  \right\rbrace$ are the angular power spectrum coefficients associated to $\TG$, and $\langle x, y \rangle$ is the great circle distance between \(x\) and \(y\), the arc length of the great circle connecting the two points. The angular power spectrum is the collection of the coefficients for the Legendre expansion of the covariance function of the field. Roughly speaking, it gives the expected variance of the field at angular scale roughly $\pi/\ell$
such that higher $\ell$'s correspond to finer angular features; it is related to the harmonic coefficients by the following formula
\begin{equation}
\Ex{  \almG \cc{a}_{\ell^\prime,m^\prime;G} }=  \ClG \delta_{\ell}^{\ell^\prime} \delta_{m}^{m^\prime},  \label{eq:powerdef}
\end{equation}
where $\delta_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ is the Kronecker symbol.
We assume a mild regularity condition on the angular power spectrum 
$\Cl$, consistent with standard models used in applications such as cosmology and spatial statistics, see for example \cite{marpec2,dlm}.
\begin{condition}\label{cond:powerspectrum}
\label{powerspectrum}Let $\ClG$ be defined as in \eqref{eq:powerdef}. For any $\ell \geq 0$, there exist an amplitude $A>0$, and a spectral index $\alpha >2$ so that%
\begin{equation*}
\ClG=A\left(1+\ell\right) ^{-\alpha }.  \label{eq:powercond}
\end{equation*}
\end{condition}
Heuristically, Condition \ref{cond:powerspectrum} imposes a form of regular variation on the asymptotic behavior of $\ClG$, reflecting features that are central to many practical applications of spherical random fields. For instance, this condition aligns with the behavior of the Sachs-Wolfe power spectrum, which serves as a leading model for the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) radiation. The Sachs-Wolfe spectrum describes the fluctuations in the primordial gravitational potential (see, for example, \cite{dode2004}). In this framework, the constant $A$ quantifies the overall intensity of the random field, while the spectral index $\alpha$
encodes the scale invariance properties of the field, offering insight into its underlying structure and physical characteristics. This balance between intensity and scale invariance is critical for understanding and modeling phenomena such as the CMB. 
You can smoothly add these important statistical properties of the harmonic coefficients in a dedicated paragraph after you introduce the angular power spectrum and its relation to the harmonic coefficients via \eqref{eq:powerdef}. 
Under the assumption of isotropy and Gaussianity, the harmonic coefficients $\lbrace \almG: \ell \geq 1, m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell \rbrace$ inherit a particularly convenient probabilistic structure. First, they are mutually independent, and, considering also that $T_G$ is centered, each $\almG$ is a centered complex Gaussian random variable with variance $\ClG$:
\begin{equation*}
	\almG \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \ClG), \quad \mbox{independently for all } \ell \geq 1,; m = -\ell, \ldots, \ell,
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{N}(0,\ClG)$ denotes a complex Gaussian distribution with zero mean and variance $\ClG$ (see e.g., \cite{MaPeCUP,ls15}).
Then, under Gaussianity and isotropy, the harmonic coefficients $\lbrace a_{\ell m;G}: \ell \geq 1, m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell \rbrace$ form a collection of mutually independent, centered complex Gaussian random variables with variances given by \eqref{eq:powerdef}. Following for example \cite{MaPeCUP}[Chapter 5], we can also show that all odd-order joint moments vanish, that is, for any integer $k$,
\begin{equation*}
	\mathbb{E} \left[ \prod_{ j=1}^{2k+1} a_{\ell_j, m_j;G}  \right] = 0;
\end{equation*}
while all even-order joint moments decompose as sums over pairwise contractions:
\begin{equation*} %\label{eq:wick}
	\mathbb{E} \left[ a_{\ell_1 ,m_1;G} \ldots a_{\ell_{2k} m_{2k};G} \right]
	= \sum_{\pi \in \mathcal{P}_{2k}} \prod_{(i,j) \in \pi}
	\mathbb{E} \left[ a_{\ell_i ,m_i;G}  \bar a_{\ell_j, -m_j;G} \right],
\end{equation*}
where the sum runs over all pairwise partitions $\pi$ of the index set $\lbrace 1,\ldots,2k\brace$, and each contraction term vanishes unless $\ell_i = \ell_j$ and $m_i = -m_j$.
This formula is a complex version of Wick’s theorem, valid for centered complex Gaussian variables (see \cite{marpec4}). It plays a central role in the analysis of high-order statistics, bispectra and polyspectra of isotropic fields on the sphere.
  \subsection{Clebsch-Gordan coefficients and Wigner symbols}\label{sec:wigner}
  In this section, we collect some concepts and results concerning Wigner symbols and the Clebsch-Gordan coefficients, which will be crucial to understand the behavior of perturbed Gaussian random fields. For further details, the reader is referred to \cite{vmk}. 
%  The  Wigner $3j$ symbols are defined as
%  \begin{align*}
%  	& \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{\mm{3}}\\& \quad \quad \quad \quad \quad = \left(-  1  \right)^{\el{2}+\mm{2}+\el{3}+\mm{3}} \left[ \frac{\left(\el{3}+\mm{3}\right)!\left(\el{3}-\mm{3}\right)!} {\left(\el{1} +  \mm{1}\right)!\left(\el{1}  -\mm{1}\right)!\left(\el{2} +\mm{2}\right)!\left(\el{2} \! -\mm{2}\right)!} \!\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}\\
%  	& \quad \quad \quad \quad \quad \quad \left[\frac{\left(\el{1}+\el{2}-\el{3}\right)!\left(\el{1}-\el{2}+\el{3}\right)! \left(-\el{1}+\el{2}+\el{3}\right)!}{\left(\el{1}+\el{2}+\el{3}+1\right)!}\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}	\\ 
%  	&\quad \quad \quad \quad \quad \quad \sum_{z} \frac{\left(-1\right)^z \left(\el{2}  +\el{3} +\mm{1} -z\right)!\left(\el{1}-\mm{1}+z\right)!}{z! \left(\el{2}  +\el{3} \! +\el{1}  -z\right)!\left(\el{3}  +\mm{3}  -z\right)!\left(\el{1}-\el{2}-\mm{3} +z\right)!},
%  \end{align*}
%  where the summation involves all $z$'s such that the factorials are non-negative. 
Instead of detailing the full definition of the Wigner $3j$ symbol, involving intricate combinatorial expressions, we note that it encodes the coupling rules for angular momenta in quantum mechanics and plays a central role in enforcing selection rules for spherical harmonic triples. In our context, it will effectively determines whether the bispectrum contributions vanish or not, based on the triangle condition among multipoles and parity constraints. More in detail, according to \cite[Proposition 3.44]{MaPeCUP}, the Wigner $3j$ symbols are nonzero only when the following conditions are satisfied::
  \begin{enumerate}
  	\item the Wigner $3j$ coefficients are nonzero only if the triangle conditions 
  	\begin{align}\label{eq:ell}
  		&	\abs{\el{i}-\el{j}} \leq
  		\el{k} \leq \el{i} +\el{j}, \quad i\neq j \neq k = 1, 2, 3\\
  		& m_1 + m_2 + m_3 = 0;\label{eq:m}
  	\end{align}
  	\item the Wigner $3j$ coefficients are invariant under
  	permutations of any two column when the sum $\el{1}+\el{2}+\el{3}$ is even; 
  	\item the following upper bound holds for any $\el{1}, \el{2}, \el{3}$:
  	\begin{align}\label{eq:wignerup}
  		\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{m_1}{m_2}{m_3}\leq 
  		\left( \max\left[\left(2\el{1}+1\right)\left(2\el{2}+1\right)\left(2\el{3}+1\right)\right] \right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}.
  	\end{align}
  	\item If the triangle conditions holds and $m_1 = m_2 = m_3 = 0$, the
  	Wigner $3j$ coefficients are nonzero only if the sum $\el{1} + \el{2} + \el{3}$ is even;
  	\item For every choice of $\el{1}, \el{2}, \el{3}$, the following sign inversion rule holds 
  	\begin{align*}	
  		\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{m_1}{m_2}{m_3}
  		=    \left( -1\right)^{\el{1}+\el{2}+\el{3}} 	\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{-{m_1}}{-{m_2}}{-{m_3}}.
  	\end{align*}
  \end{enumerate}
  The Clebsch-Gordan coefficients $\cg$ are defined from the Wigner $3j$-symbols thanks to the following identities 
  \begin{align}
  	& \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{m} = \left(-1\right)^{\ell+m} \frac{1}{\sqrt{\lcard}} \cgbis{\ell}{m}{\el{1}}{-\mm{1}}{\el{2}}{-\mm{2}},\notag \\ %\label{eq:wigner}\\
  	&\cg = \left(-1\right)^{\el{1}-\el{2}+m} \sqrt{\lcard} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{-m}.\label{eq:wignercj}
  \end{align}
  Loosely speaking, the Clebsch-Gordan coefficients $\cg$ can be viewed as elements of the unitary Clebsch Gordan matrices $C_{\ell_1,\ell2}$,  which provide an explicit direct sum decomposition of the tensor product of two irreducible representations for $SO\left(3\right)$ (see, for further details, \cite{MaPeCUP,vmk}).\\
  Here, we review some properties of Clebsch-Gordan coefficients that are essential, among others, for achieving the main results of this paper:
  \begin{align}
  	&\cgbis{\ell^\prime}{m^\prime}{\ell_2}{m_2}{\ell_1}{m_1}=(-1)^{\ell_1+\ell_2-\ell}\cgbis{\ell^\prime}{m^\prime}{\ell_1}{m_1}{\ell_2}{m_2}\label{eq:prop0}\\
  	& \sum_{m_1}(-1)^{\ell_1-m_1}\cgbis{\ell}{0}{\ell_1}{m_1}{\ell_1}{-m_1} = \sqrt{2\ell_1+1} \delta_{0}^{\ell} \label{eq:prop1}\\
  	&\cgbis{\ell}{0}{\ell_1}{m_1}{\ell_2}{-m_2} = \frac{(-1)^{\ell_1-m_1} \delta_{\ell_2}^{\ell_1}\delta_{m_2}^{m_1}}{\sqrt{2\ell_1+1} } \label{eq:prop2},\\
  	&\sum_{m_1,m_2} \cg\cgbis{\ell^\prime}{m^\prime}{\ell_1}{m_1}{\ell_2}{m_2}=\delta_{\ell}^{\ell^\prime}\delta_{m}^{m^\prime} \label{eq:prop3},
  \end{align}
  see \cite[Eq 8.4.3 p.245]{vmk}, \cite[Eq 8.7.1 p.259]{vmk}, \cite[Eq 8.5.1 p.248]{vmk}, and \cite[Eq 8.7.2 p.259]{vmk} respectively.\\
  Finally, we can introduce the so-called Gaunt integral, namely, the integral of the product among three spherical harmonics, which is a pivotal tool to develop harmonic analysis of perturbed Gaussian random fields. \\
  The Gaunt integral $\gaunt$ can be defined in terms of either Wigner $3j$ symbols or Clebsch-Gordan coefficients as follows
  \begin{align}
  	\notag	& \gaunt  = \int_{\sphere} \Ybis{1}{1}\left( x \right)\Ybis{2}{2}\left( x \right)\Yconj \left( x \right) \diff x\\
  	& \quad\quad\quad =\left(-1\right)^{m} \sqrt{\frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)\left(\lcard\right)}{4\pi}} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{-m} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{0}{0}{0} \notag \\
  	& \quad \quad\quad= \sqrt{\frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)}{4\pi\left(\lcard\right)}} \cgbis{\ell}{m}{\el{1}}{\mm{1}}{\el{2}}{\mm{2}}\cgnull. \label{eq:gaunt}
  \end{align}
  \subsection{Perturbed random fields and their stochastic properties}
With perturbed Gaussian random fields, we denote those random fields,  where non-Gaussianity, arises as a ``small'' perturbation of the Gaussian random field. This model finds relevant applications in Cosmology and Astrophysics, among other research fields. Indeed, most of the inflationary models predict the fluctuations of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) radiation to be close to but not exactly Gaussian  (see, for example, \cite{planck2015b} and the references therein). From now on, we will focus on the so-called ``slow-roll'' inflationary model \cite{dode2004}. Thus, the perturbed Gaussian random field can be parametrized in the simplest form by the following model 
\begin{align*}
T\left( x\right)&=\TG\left( x\right) +\fNL \left( \TG^2\left( x\right) - \Ex{\TG^2\left( x\right)} \right) \text{ , }x\in \sphere  \\
& =T_{G}\left( x\right) +\fNL H_{2}\left( T_{G}\left(
	x\right) \right) \text{ , }x\in \mathbb{S}^{2}.  \label{nongaussianfield}
\end{align*}
where $T(x)$ describes the primordial curvature perturbation field at the end of inflation and $T_{G}$ is its Gaussian part, while $H_{2}$ is the Hermite polynomial of order $2$. The non-Gaussian random field is obtained by summing to the Gaussian random field $T_G$ a perturbation whose amplitude is given by the parameter $\fNL$. The perturbation itself depends on $T_G$. It corresponds indeed to the second-order Hermite polynomial $H_2\left(u\right)=u^2-1$ taking as argument $T_G$ itself.\\
The dimensionless parameter $\fNL$ describes the amplitude of the non-Gaussian perturbation and, in general, depends only upon physical constants (see again \cite{dode2004}). The term $\fNL \Ex{T^2_{G}\left( x\right)}$ is expected to be very small, more precisely of order $10^{-3}/10^{-4}$ (see, for instance, \cite{bartolo}). \\
 Let us now focus on the harmonic expansion of $T$.  The following equality holds, in the $L^2$-sense,
 \begin{equation}%\notag
 	\begin{split}
 T\left(x\right)& =\suml \summ \alm \Y \left(x\right) \\
 &	\label{eq:Texpansion}= \suml \summ \left(\almG \left(x\right) + \fNL \almtwo\right) \Y \left(x\right),
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
where the  harmonic coefficients $\left\lbrace \alm:\ell \geq 1, m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell \right\rbrace$ are defined by
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:Tharmonic}
 \alm=\int_{\sphere}T\left( x\right) \Yconj \left( x\right) \diff x= \almG + \fNL  \almtwo.
 \end{equation}%
The Fourier expansion of $H_{2}\left( T_{G}\left(
x\right) \right) =T_{G}^{2}\left( x\right) -1$ is given by 
\begin{equation*}
H_{2}\left( T_{G}\left( x\right) \right) =\suml \summ \almtwo \Y, \quad x\in \mathbb{S}^{2},
\end{equation*}%
where%
\begin{align*}
\almtwo &= \int_{\sphere} \left( T_{G}^{2}\left( x\right)
-1\right) \Yconj  \left( x\right) \diff x \\
%&= \sum_{\ell _{1}\geq 1} \sum_{\ell _{2}\geq 1} \sum_{m_{1}=-\ell_{1}}^{\ell_{1}}  \sum_{m_{2}=-\ell_{2}}^{\ell_{2}}   a_{\ell_1,m_1;G} a_{\ell_2,m_2;G} \int_{\sphere} \Ybis{1}{1} \left( x\right) \Ybis{2}{2} \left( x\right) \Yconj \left( x\right) \diff x \\
& = \sum_{\ell _{1}\geq 1} \sum_{\ell _{2}\geq 1} \sum_{m_{1}=-\ell_{1}}^{\ell_{1}}  \sum_{m_{2}=-\ell_{2}}^{\ell_{2}}   \almbis{1}{1} \almbis{2}{2} \left(-1\right)^m \gauntbis ,
\end{align*}
where the Gaunt integral, given by Equation \eqref{eq:gaunt}, quantifies the overlap of three spherical harmonics. Linking the Gaunt integral with both Wigner $3j$-symbols and Clebsch-Gordan coefficients (see Section \ref{sec:wigner} above) allows us the  following representations for $a_{\ell,m;2}$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} \almtwo& = \sum_{\ell _{1},\ell _{2}\geq 0}  \sum_{m_{1},m_{2}}   a_{\ell_1,m_1;G} a_{\ell_2,m_2;G}  \sqrt{\frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)}{4\pi \left(\lcard\right)}}   \cg \cgnull\\
& =\sum_{\ell _{1},\ell _{2}\geq 0}  \sum_{m_{1},m_{2}} \left(-1\right)^m a_{\ell_1,m_1;G} a_{\ell_2,m_2;G}  \sqrt{\frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)\left(\lcard\right)}{4\pi}} \\
\label{eq:almtwo} &\times \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{m_{1}}{m_{2}}{-m} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{0}{0}{0}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We now review some well-established results concerning the stocastic properties of $\lbrace\almtwo:\ell \geq1, m=-\ell, \ldots, \ell \rbrace$; for additional information, the reader is referred for example to \cite{m,MaPeCUP} and the references therein. 
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:almtwo}
	For any $\ell \geq 1$, and $m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell$, let $a_{\ell,m;2}$ be defined by Equation \eqref{eq:almtwo}.
	Then it holds that 
	\begin{align*}
		& \Ex{a_{\ell,m;2}}=0;\\% \sqrt{4\pi}\delta_{\ell}^{0} \delta_{m}^{0}\sum_{\ell^\prime\geq 1} \frac{2\ell^\prime+1}{4\pi} C_{\ell^\prime;G}\\
		& \Cov{a_{\ell,m;2}}{a_{\ell^\prime,m^\prime;2}} =C_{\ell;2} \delta^{\ell}_{\ell^{\prime}}\delta^{m}_{m^{\prime}},
	\end{align*}
	where 
	\begin{equation*}
		C_{\ell;2} =  2\sum_{\ell _{1},\ell _{2}}  \frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)}{4\pi\left(\lcard\right)} \left(\cgnull\right)^2C_{\ell_1;G}C_{\ell_2;G}.
		\end{equation*}
		Also, for any $x,y \in \sphere$, it holds that 
		\begin{align*}
		&	\Ex{H_2\left(T_G(x)\right)} = \sum_{\ell \geq 1} \frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell;G};\\
		&	\Cov{H_2\left(T_G(x)\right)}{H_2\left(T_G(y)\right)} =\sum_{\ell \geq 1} \frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell;2} P_{\ell}\left(\langle x,y \rangle\right).
		\end{align*}
\end{proposition}
The proof of this Proposition is a shortened version of the one provided by \cite{m,MaPeCUP} and is reported in Section \ref{sec:proofs}; here, we simply note that by transitioning from Clebsch-Gordan coefficients to Wigner $3j$ symbols, the following identity holds:
\begin{equation*}
C_{\ell;2}=2\sum_{\ell _{1}\geq 1} \sum_{\ell _{2}\geq 1}  \Clbis{1}\Clbis{2} \frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)\left(\lcard\right)}{4\pi} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\ell}{0}{0}{0}^2.
\end{equation*}
The following proposition compiles several results on the stochastic properties of $T$, drawn from \cite{m,MaPeCUP}.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:complete}
	Let $\alm$ and $T$ be defined as in Equations \eqref{eq:Tharmonic} and \eqref{eq:Texpansion}. Then, for any $\ell \geq 1$ and $m=-\ell, \ldots, \ell$, it holds that
	\begin{align*}
	&	\Ex{\alm}  = 0; \\% \sqrt{4\pi} \fNL \delta_{\ell}^{0}\delta_{m}^{0} \sum_{\ell^\prime>1}\frac{2\ell^\prime+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell^\prime;G} \\
	&	\Cov{\alm}{a_{\ell^\prime,m^\prime}} = C_\ell \delta_{\ell}^{\ell^\prime}\delta_{m}^{m^\prime},
	\end{align*}
	where
	\begin{equation*}
		C_{\ell}= C_{\ell;G} + \fNL^2 C_{\ell;2}.
	\end{equation*}
	Also, for any $x,y \in \mathbb{S}^2$, it holds that
	\begin{align*}
		&\Ex{T(x)}= 0; \\%\fNL  \sum_{\ell>1}\frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell;G};\\
		&\Cov{T(x)}{T(y)} = \sum_{\ell \geq 1} \frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi} C_{\ell} P_{\ell}\left(\langle x,y\rangle\right). 
	\end{align*}
\end{proposition} 
From this point onward, we will use the rescaled version of the Fourier coefficients, defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:walm}
\walm =\frac{\alm}{\sqrt{\ClG}}=\frac{\almG+\fNL \almtwo}{\sqrt{\ClG}},
\end{equation}%
while, for any $x \in \sphere$,  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wT}
	\wT(x) = \sum_{\ell \geq 0} \sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell} \walm \Y(x),
\end{equation}
such that the following corollary holds.
\begin{corollary}
	For any $\ell \geq 1$, $m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell$, let $\walm$ be defined as in Equation \eqref{eq:walm} 
\	\begin{align*}
&	\Ex{\walm}  =0; \\ % \sqrt{\frac{4\pi}{C_{0;G}}} \fNL \delta_{\ell}^{0}\delta_{m}^{0} \sum_{\ell^\prime>1}\frac{2\ell^\prime+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell^\prime;G} \\
&	\Cov{\alm}{a_{\ell^\prime,m^\prime}} = 1 +  \fNL^2 \frac{C_{\ell;2}}{\ClG}.
\end{align*}
Also, for any $x,y \in \mathbb{S}^2$, it holds that
\begin{align*}
&\Ex{\wT(x)}= 0;\\%\frac{\fNL} {\sqrt{C_{0,G}}} \sum_{\ell^\prime>1}\frac{2\ell^\prime+1}{4\pi}C_{\ell^\prime;G};\\
&\Cov{\wT(x)}{\wT(y)} = \sum_{\ell \geq 1} \frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi} \left( 1 + \fNL^2\frac{C_{\ell;2}}{C_{\ell;G}}\right) P_{\ell}\left(\langle x,y\rangle\right). 
\end{align*}
\end{corollary}
The proof follows directly the one of Proposition \ref{prop:complete} and it is here omitted for the sake of brevity.\\
\subsection{The angular bispectrum and its statistical properties}
The angular bispectrum is a pivotal tool in the analysis of cosmological data, offering valuable insights into the statistical properties of fields on the celestial sphere. It characterizes the three-point correlation function of spherical harmonics and serves as a key measure of non-Gaussianity, capturing deviations from purely Gaussian behavior in the underlying field. Analogous to how the angular power spectrum acts as the Laplace transform of the two-point angular correlation function, the angular bispectrum can be regarded as the harmonic transform of the three-point angular correlation function. More in detail, let $x_1,x_2,x_3 \in \sphere$ and $$\wT\left(x\right)=\suml \summ \walm \Y\left(x\right)$$ be a perturbed random field defined as in \eqref{eq:wT}. Also, we introduce some auxiliary notation that will be central to organizing the structure of our estimator. 
\begin{itemize}
	\item\textit{Symmetric covariance product $\Gamma_{\ell _{1}\ell _{2}\ell _{3}}$.} For any pair of distinct indexes $i\neq j=1,2,3$, define the normalized covariance product
\begin{equation*}
	\gamma_{\ell_{i},\ell_{j}} =2\frac{C_{\ell _{i}} C_{\ell_{j}}} { \sqrt{C_{\ell _{1}}C_{\ell _{2}}C_{\ell _{3}}}},
\end{equation*}
which captures the contribution of each pairwise interaction between the multipoles $\left(\ell_1,\ell,2,\ell_3\right)$ relative to the geometric mean of the full triplet. This allows us to define the combined weight
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:Gammal}
	\Gammal = 	\gamma_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}+	\gamma_{\ell_{1},\ell_{3}}+	\gamma_{\ell_{2},\ell_{3}},
\end{equation*}
which serves as a compact way to encode the total interaction strength between all three legs of the bispectrum.
\item\textit{Azimuthal Multiplicity Index $h_{\ell _{1}\ell _{2}\ell _{3}}$.} To account for the dimensional multiplicity of the spherical harmonics at each multipole 
$\ell$
\begin{equation*}\label{hal}
	\hal=\sqrt{\frac{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)\left(\lcardbis{3}\right)}{\left(4\pi\right)^3}}.
\end{equation*}%
\end{itemize}
Finally, we combine the formerly defined elements with the Wigner $3j$-symbol $\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{0}{0}{0}$to define:
\begin{itemize}
	\item  \textit{Angular Bispectrum Weight.}
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	\etal &=2\fact\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{0}{0}{0} \frac{C_{\ell _{1}}C_{\ell
			_{2}}+C_{\ell _{1}}C_{\ell _{3}}+C_{\ell _{2}}C_{\ell _{3}}}{\sqrt{C_{\ell
				_{1}}C_{\ell _{2}}C_{\ell _{3}}}}\\ &\quad\frac{\sqrt{\left(\lcardbis{1}\right)\left(\lcardbis{2}\right)\left(\lcardbis{3}\right)}}{\left(4\pi\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}}    \\
	&=2\fact\wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{0}{0}{0}\hal\Gammal\label{eq:eta},
	\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\fact= 1 + \delta_{\ell_1}^{\ell_2}+\delta_{\ell_2}^{\ell_3}+3\delta_{\ell_1}^{\ell_3}$ (see for example \cite{MaPeCUP}). 
\end{itemize}
The three point correlation is given by
\begin{align}
\nonumber & \Ex{\wT\left(x_1\right)\wT\left(x_2\right)\wT\left(x_3\right)} \\ 
 &\quad =\sum_{\ell _{1}\geq 1}\sum_{\ell _{2}\geq 1}\sum_{\ell _{3}\geq 1} \sum_{\mm{1}=-\el{1}}^{\el{1}} \sum_{\mm{2}=-\el{2}}^{\el{2}} \sum_{\mm{3}=-\el{3}}^{\el{3}} 	B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}} \Ybis{1}{1}\left(x_1\right) \Ybis{2}{2}\left(x_2\right) \Ybis{3}{3}\left(x_3\right) \label{eq:spectdef},
\end{align}
where the full bispectrum $B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fullbis}
	B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}=\Ex{\walmbis{1}{1} \walmbis{2}{2} \walmbis{3}{3}}.
\end{equation}
If we consider the non-perturbed normalized random field $\widetilde{T}_G=T_G/\sqrt{C_{\ell;G}}$,
it holds that
\begin{equation*}
B_{G; \el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{C_{\ell_1;G}C_{\ell_2;G}C_{\ell_3;G}}}\Ex{a_{\ell_1,m_1;G} a_{\ell_2,m_2;G} a_{\ell_3,m_3;G}}=0,
\end{equation*}
since the expectation of the product of an odd number of harmonic coefficients in a Gaussian field is always zero.
As a result, the three-point correlation function is also zero, expressed as:
\begin{align*} \Ex{T_G\left(x_1\right) T_G\left(x_2\right) T_G\left(x_3\right)} = 0,
\end{align*} 
see for example \cite{m, MaPeCUP}.\\
Assume now that the considered random field $T$ is isotropic; in this case, both the sides of \eqref{eq:spectdef} are not modified by rotations of the coordinate system. it implies that the structure of $B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}$ ensures that  $\Ex{T\left(x_1\right) T\left(x_2\right)T\left(x_3\right)}$ remains unaltered if $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ 
are rotated by the same angles. Following  \cite{m}, the angular bispectrum of an isotropic field can be not-null only if the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\el{1}$, $\el{2}$, and $\el{3}$ satisfy the triangle rule, that is, $\abs{\el{j}-\el{k}}\leq\el{i} \leq \el{j} + \el{k}$, for all choices of
$i, j, k = 1, 2, 3$;
\item $\el{1}+\el{2}+\el{3}$ is even;
\item $\mm{1} + \mm{2} + \mm{3} = 0$.
 \end{enumerate}
 These selection rules directly mirror the properties of the Wigner $3j$-symbols, which encode the angular momentum coupling conditions. In particular, the bispectrum coefficient $B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}$ can be nonzero only if the corresponding Wigner $3j$-symbol is nonvanishing, which precisely requires the triangle condition, the sum $\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}+\ell_{3}$ to be even, and $\mm{1}+\mm{2}+\mm{3}=0$ (see Section \ref{sec:wigner}).\\
The full bispectrum is rarely used directly in cosmological analyses due to its detailed nature; instead, rotationally invariant forms are preferred. The rotationally invariant version of the bispectrum is obtained by summing over all 
azimuth indices the full bispectrum weighted by the related Wigner 
$3j$-symbol to enforce rotational symmetry:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rotbis}
	\begin{split}
		B_{\el{1},\el{3},\el{3}}&=\sum_{m_1,m_2,m_3} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{\mm{3}} B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}\\
		& =\sum_{m_1,m_2,m_3} \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{\mm{1}}{\mm{2}}{\mm{3}} \Ex{\walmbis{1}{1} \walmbis{2}{2}\walmbis{3}{3}}.
	\end{split}
\end{equation} 
The rotationally invariant bispectrum encodes the overall strength of mode coupling in harmonic space; it still
satisfies the triangle condition for $\el{1},\el{2}$, and $\el{3}$, and includes both physical information and geometric factors via the Wigner $3j$-symbol. The dependence on $\mm{1},\mm{2}, \mm{3}$, which does not carry any physical information if the field is isotropic, is removed. This representation is widely used in theoretical and observational cosmology to study non-Gaussianities and their relation to physical processes.\\
Finally, the reduced bispectrum is defined to isolate the physical signal by factoring out the geometric terms (e.g., Wigner $3j$-symbol and related factors) from the rotationally invariant bispectrum $B_{\el{1},\el{3},\el{3}}$.
More in detail, as shown by \cite{hu}, a necessary and sufficient condition for $B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}}$ to represent the angular bispectrum of an isotropic random field 
is that there exists a real function $b_{\el{1}, \el{2},\el{3}}$, symmetric with respect to the permutations of the three indexes $\el{1}$, $\el{2}$ and $\el{3}$, such that the following identity holds:
\begin{align*}
%& B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}}^{\mm{1},\mm{2},\mm{3}} = \mathcal{G}\left(\el{1},\mm{1};\el{2},\mm{2};\el{3},\mm{3}\right) b_{\el{1}, \el{2},\el{3}};\\
& B_{\el{1},\el{2},\el{3}} = \wigner{\el{1}}{\el{2}}{\el{3}}{0}{0}{0} b_{\el{1}, \el{2},\el{3}}.
\end{align*}
The function $b_{\el{1}, \el{2},\el{3}}$ is called reduced bispectrum and it features the advantage to be independent on the indexes $m_1, m_2$ and $m_3$. 
Using the properties of the Wigner $3j$ symbols composing the Gaunt integral, yields the consequence that $\mathcal{G}\left(\el{1},\mm{1};\el{2},\mm{2};\el{3},\mm{3}\right)$ is not null only if the three conditions listed above are fulfilled. \\
As outlined in \cite[Section 9.2.2]{MaPeCUP}, the full bispectrum can similarly be expressed in terms of the other two representations as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:expwalm}
	\begin{split}
\mathbb{E}\left[ \widetilde{a}_{\ell _{1},m_{1}}\widetilde{a}_{\ell _{2},m_{2}}%
\widetilde{a}_{\ell _{3},m_{3}}\right] & =%
\begin{pmatrix}
\ell _{1} & \ell _{2} & \ell _{3} \\ 
m_{1} & m_{2} & m_{3}%
\end{pmatrix}%
\Bisp \\
& =
\begin{pmatrix}
\ell _{1} & \ell _{2} & \ell _{3} \\ 
m_{1} & m_{2} & m_{3}%
\end{pmatrix}%
\begin{pmatrix}
\ell _{1} & \ell _{2} & \ell _{3} \\ 
0 & 0 & 0%
\end{pmatrix}%
\bisp,
\end{split}
\end{equation}%
the reader is also referred for further details to \cite{m,marpec4} and the references therein.\\
Let us now study the full bispectrum related to a perturbed random field $T$. 
The following result, as originally presented in \cite{m}, examines the expected value of the product of three harmonic coefficients in a perturbed field. 
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:expect}
Let $\walm$ be defined  as in  Equation \eqref{eq:walm}. Then, it holds that 
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathbb{E}\left[ \widetilde{a}_{\ell _{1},m_{1}}\widetilde{a}_{\ell_{2},m_{2}}\widetilde{a}_{\ell _{3},m_{3}}\right]	= \fNL \etal + o_{\ell}(\fNL). 
	\end{equation*}
%	Additionally, when including the case $\ell_i=0$, $i=1,2,3$, there is an extra term given by
%	\begin{equation*}
%		\fNL\frac{1}{C_{0;G}} \sum_{\lambda \geq 1} \frac{2\lambda+1}{\sqrt{4\pi}} C_{\lambda;G} . 
%	\end{equation*}		
%	This additional summand applies specifically for any $\ell_i=0$.
\end{proposition}
Combining equations \eqref{eq:eta} and \eqref{eq:rotbis} yields an explicit expression linking the bispectrum to its associated angular bispectral weight:
\begin{align}\label{eq:Bisp}
 \Bisp = &  \fNL \etal + o\left(\fNL\right) %\\
% \bisp = & 2 \fNL \hal ^ {\frac{1}{2}} \Gammal +o\left(\fNL\right) .
\end{align}
A natural estimator for the bispectrum can be derived directly from Equation \eqref{eq:rotbis} and is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Bispest}
\Bispest =\sum_{m_{1}=-\el{1}}^{\el{1}}\sum_{m_{2}=-\el{2}}^{\el{2}}\sum_{m_{3}=-\el{3}}^{\el{3}} \wigner{\ell _{1}}{\ell _{2}} {\ell _{3}}{m_{1}} {m_{2}} {m_{3}} \
\widetilde{a}_{\ell _{1},m_{1}}\widetilde{a}_{\ell _{2},m_{2}}\widetilde{a}%
_{\ell _{3},m_{3}}.
\end{equation}%
Comprehensive details regarding the stochastic properties of this estimator are thoroughly discussed in \cite{m,MaPeCUP}. In this section, we limit our focus to summarizing the key properties directly related to the expectation and variance of the estimator, which are relevant for the context of our study. 
\begin{remark}[The choice of the index set]
From now on, we restrict attention to ordered indices $\left(\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3\right)$ satisfying $\ell_1 <\ell_2<\ell_3$ rather than considering all possible combinations. This restriction is justified by the symmetry of the bispectrum: due to the permutation invariance of the Wigner $3j$-symbols and the isotropy of the field, the bispectrum remains unchanged under any reordering of the multipole indices. As a result, considering all permutations leads to redundant information. By focusing on the ordered case, one selects a unique representative from each equivalence class of index triples, thereby avoiding overcounting and significantly reducing computational complexity. Furthermore, this restriction simplifies statistical analysis by ensuring that distinct bispectrum configurations are non-redundant and more easily treated as statistically independent or weakly correlated. This approach is both analytically and computationally efficient, without any loss of generality. 
\end{remark}
These results collected and presented in the following proposition have been originally shown in \cite{m} (see also \cite[Theorem 9.7]{MaPeCUP}).
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Best}
For any triple $\left( \ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3\right)\in\mathbb{N}^3$ such that $\ell_1 < \ell_2 < \ell_3$, let $\Bispest$ be defined as in Equation \eqref{eq:Bispest}. Then it holds that
\begin{align}
&\Ex{ \Bispest} = \Bisp; \label{eq:exb};\\
&\Ex{\Bispest^2} = 1 + O\left(\fNL^2\right)\label{eq:varb};\\
& \cumq{\Bispest} = 6\left(\wignersix{\ell_1}{\ell_2}{\ell_3}{\ell_1}{\ell_2}{\ell_3}+\sum_{i=1}^{3}\frac{1}{2\ell_i+1} \right) + O\left(\fNL\right). \label{eq:cqb}
\end{align}
Also, it holds that 
\begin{equation*}
\cumq{\Bispest} \leq \frac{12}{2\ell_1+1}.
\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[Bispectrum and spherical white noise]\label{rem:chaos}
We recall also an alternative derivation for the bispectrum based the definition
of spherical stochastic processes (see, for example, \cite{MaPeCUP,yadrenko}). The spherical
white noise on the sphere $W\left( \diff x\right) $ is a stochastic process featuring
the following properties:
\begin{align*}
& \Ex{ W\left( \diff x\right) } = 0; \\
& \Ex{ W\left( \diff x_{1}\right) \cc{W}\left( \diff x_{2}\right) }
=\diff x_{1} \delta \left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) ,
\end{align*}%
where $\delta \left( \cdot \right) $ is the Dirac delta function. The
component of a random field corresponding to the multipole $\ell$ is given
by%
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{split}
