\section{The construction of the OLS estimator for the nonlinearity parameter}\label{sec:OLS}

Let us assume the following experimental set-up. We fix a parameter $r \in \left(0,1/2\right)$ and a maximum angular resolution $L\in \mathbb{N}$, corresponding to the upper multipole level; we also define $L_0=rL$. Without losing generality, we consider also $L_0 \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume that the array %$\left\left\lbrace\walm: \ell=1,\ldots L; m = −\ell,\ldots,\ell\right\right\rbrace$
\begin{equation*}
\left\lbrace\walm:\ell=L_0,\ldots,L;m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell \right\rbrace
\end{equation*}
 is evaluated from the observed
field $T$. Furthermore, we assume that the set of harmonic coefficients from $L_0$ to $L$ can be reconstructed without numerical error or observational noise. The value of $L$ clearly grows larger depending on the precision of the considered experiment. For instance, $L$ is in the order of $500/600$, $1500/2000$, or $10 000–12 000$ for data collected from WMAP, Planck, South Pole Telescope SPT and the Simons' Observatory SO/SO4, respectively. \\
In what follows, we restrict our attention to the set of ordered triples
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LambdaL}
	\LambdaL = \lbrace \left(\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3\right): L_0\leq \ell_1 < \ell_2 < \ell_3 \leq L; \ell_2-\ell_1\leq \ell_3 \leq \ell_1+ \ell_2 \rbrace
\end{equation}
when analyzing the bispectrum. This choice is motivated by both mathematical and computational considerations. The bispectrum is invariant under permutations of the multipole indices due to the symmetry of the Wigner $3j$-symbols and the isotropy of the underlying random field, meaning that unordered configurations yield redundant information. By focusing on strictly ordered triples, we avoid this redundancy, reduce the computational cost, and simplify the statistical treatment of the bispectrum. Also, no generality is lost, as the full information content is preserved. The additional triangle inequality constraint ensures the non-vanishing of the associated Wigner coefficients, thereby identifying only admissible configurations.
\begin{remark}[The cardinality of $\LambdaL$]\label{rem:Lambda}
Let $\NL$ be the cardinality of $\LambdaL$. For large $L$ the dominant behavior of the sum is cubic in  $L$, but the triangle inequality excludes many triples.
Indeed the total number of  triples is 
\[ \binom{L(1-r)+1}{3} \approx \frac{L^3\left(1-r\right)^3}{6}. \] 
The triangle inequality cuts off roughly about half of them, since in each random triple, the sum of the two smaller indices doesn't always exceed the third, such that 
\begin{equation*}
	\NL\approx \frac{L^3\left(1-r\right)^3}{12}.
\end{equation*}	 
Indeed, rescaling $x_i = \ell_i/L$,  such that $ x_i=\left(r,1\right)$
 and look at the region. The region where $x_3<x_1+x_2$  has volume
\begin{equation*}
	\int_{r}^{1}\int_{x_2}^{1} (1-x_2)\diff x_1 \diff x_2 = \frac{1}{6}-\frac{r}{2}+\frac{r^2}{2}-\frac{r^3}{6},
\end{equation*}
while the volume of the region where the triangle inequality holds is given by
\begin{equation*}
	\int_{r}^{1}\int_{x_2}^{1} \min\left(1,x_1+x_2\right)-x_2 \diff x_1 \diff x_2 = \frac{1}{12}-\frac{r^2}{2}+\frac{r^3}{2}.
\end{equation*}
For small $r$, the ratio between the two volumes reduces to $1/2$.
\end{remark}
\subsection{Ordinary least squares method and nonlinearity parameter}
In our approach, we treat the non-Gaussian amplitude $\fNL$ as the slope in simple linear regression of the observed bispectrum against its theoretical counterpart, and we estimate it by ordinary least squares (OLS). Concretely, for any $\left(\ell_1, \ell_2,\ell_3\right) \in \LambdaL$, we stack the measured, filtered bispectrum  coefficients  $\lbrace\Bispest: \left(\ell_1, \ell_2,\ell_3\right) \in \LambdaL \rbrace$ into a data vector and the corresponding model predictions $\lbrace\etal: \left(\ell_1, \ell_2,\ell_3\right) \in \LambdaL \rbrace$
into a design vector; then the OLS estimator $\fNLest$ is exactly the best linear unbiased estimator under Gaussian noise assumptions, recovering the Komatsu–Spergel–Wandelt (KSW) estimator structure (see \cite{ksw05}), making high-resolution analyses feasible, inheriting its $O(L^3)$ scaling and optimal variance. \\
\begin{remark}[]
		In our model, we will assume a slight dependence of $\fNL$ on $L$, namely, $\fNL = \fNL\left(L\right)$ so that   
	\begin{equation*}\label{eq:fNLcond}
		\lim_{L \rightarrow \infty} \fNL = 0.
	\end{equation*}
	This condition reflects the framework of local alternatives, where one studies the behavior of estimators under increasingly subtle deviations from Gaussianity; this is well motivated in cosmology, where leading inflationary models predict a nearly Gaussian primordial curvature field, and any nonzero $\fNL$ 
	is expected to be small—potentially vanishing in the high-resolution limit.
%	This assumption reflects the expectation that the influence of primordial non-Gaussianity becomes negligible at higher $\ell$, where smaller multipole scales are dominated by processes such as Silk damping or other secondary effects. In contrast, larger angular scales, corresponding to lower $\ell$ are more sensitive to primordial physics, making them the primary regime for detecting non-Gaussian signatures. Also, allowing $\fNL$
%	to depend on $\ell$ modifies the shape and scale dependence of the bispectrum $B_{\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell,3}$ which is then proportional to $\fNL$. More in detail, if $\fNL$ decreases as $L \rightarrow \infty$, the bispectrum contribution from configurations with high $\ell$ values will be suppressed. This results in a bispectrum that is more prominent at lower multipoles, aligning with observations of large-scale structures and the Sachs-Wolfe effect. 
Scale-dependent $\fNL$ naturally arises in inflationary scenarios, with running couplings or multi-field interactions, so that as observational resolution $L$ increases, $\fNL$,
%effectively vanishes at small scaleThese models arise naturally in scenarios where the generation of non-Gaussianity involves scale-dependent processes, such as inflationary models, where the Scale dependence of $\fNL$
%can occur due to running of parameters in the inflationary potential or interactions of multiple fields, 
see for example \cite{LSFS10} and references therein. 
\end{remark}
In what follows, we review the construction of this nonlinear estimator, and we prove a central limit theorem for 
$\fNLest-\fNL$ in Wasserstein distance by the Fourth Moment Theorem of Nourdin–Peccati \cite{noupebook}, and we derive an explicit rate of convergence. \\
More in detail, we consider the linear model for the measured bispectrum coefficients,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
\Bispest= \fNL \etal + \vel, 
\end{equation}
where the experimental noise $\left\lbrace\vel: \left(\el{1} , \el{2} , \el{3} \right) \in \LambdaL \right\rbrace$ consists in a set of $\NL$ independent and identically distributed random variables, assumed to be centered, with variance $\sigma_\varepsilon$, uncorrelated to $\Bispest$.  \\
We can now define the following vectors of dimension $\NL$ based on $\Bispest$, $\etal$ and $\vel$ as follows
\begin{equation}\label{eq:vec}
	\begin{split}
& \Yest = \left[\widehat{B}_{L_0,L_0+1,L_0+2},\ldots,\widehat{B}_{L_0,L_0+1,L},\widehat{B}_{L_0,L_0+2,L_0+3},\ldots,\widehat{B}_{L_0,L-1,L},\ldots,\widehat{B}_{L-2,L-1,L}\right]^T\\
& H = \left[\eta_{L_0,L_0+1,L_0+2},\ldots,\eta_{L_0,L_0+1,L},\eta_{L_0,L_0+2,L_0+3},\ldots,\eta_{L_0,L-1,L},\ldots,\eta_{L-2,L-1,L}\right]^T\\
& E = \left[\varepsilon_{L_0,L_0+1,L_0+2},\ldots,\varepsilon_{L_0,L_0+1,L},\varepsilon_{L_0,L_0+2,L_0+3},\ldots,\varepsilon_{L_0,L-1,L},\ldots,\varepsilon_{L-2,L-1,L}\right]^T,
\end{split} 
\end{equation} 
where $\Yest$ is the data vector containing the estimated bispectrum from observations, $H$ is the design matrix constructed from the theoretical shape template, and $E$ denotes the vector of residuals.
Thus, \eqref{eq:model} can be expressed in matrix form as
\begin{align*}
\Yest= \fNL H + E.
\end{align*}
%Let us compute the sum of the squared residuals as
%\begin{align*}
%= \summinus\varepsilon_{\ell _{1},\ell _{2},\ell _{3}}^2
% = \summinus \left(\Bispest- \fNL \etal  \right)^2,
%\end{align*}
%or, alternatively,
%$$
%E^T E = \left(\Yest-\fNL H\right)^T\left(\Yest-\fNL H\right).
%$$
Applying the ordinary least squares (OLS) method to the estimation of the non-Gaussianity parameter
$\fNL$ leads to the construction of an estimator $\fNLest$. In this framework, we aim to minimize the squared residuals between the observed bispectrum and its theoretical expectation under a linear model. Specifically, the OLS estimator is defined by the solution to the optimization problem:
\begin{equation*}
\underset{\fNL \in \mathbb{R}^+}{ \argmin} E^T E.
\end{equation*}
Using standard results from linear regression theory, the OLS solution can be explicitly written as:
\begin{equation*}
	\fNLest = \left(H^{T}H\right)^{-1}H^{T}\Yest.
%	\fNLest & = \left(H^{T}H\right)^{-1}H^{T}\Yest, 
\end{equation*}
In our context, this expression becomes explicitly:
\begin{align}
	\fNLest & = \left(\summinus \etal^2\right)^{-1} \summinus \etal  \Bispest  \label{eq:estimatordef}.
%	& = H^TH  , \Bispest,
\end{align}
%where we define the normalization factor
%\begin{equation*}\label{eq:Seta}
%	\Seta = \sum_{\ell_1 < \ell_2 < \ell_3} \etal^2.
%\end{equation*}
This estimator effectively projects the observed bispectrum onto the theoretical template, yielding an estimate for  $\fNL$ a manner analogous to an OLS regression where $H$ 
is the regressor and $Y$ is the response variable.
Thus, using the bispectrum, we can write an OLS estimator for $\fNL$ that leverages the linear relation between the observed three-point statistics and the underlying non-Gaussianity parameter.


\section{Asymptotic properties of the estimator: Gaussianity}\label{sec:main}
As mentioned in Remark \ref{rem:chaos}, since $\fNL$ is a linear combination of elements of the sample bispectrum, which belongs to the third Wiener chaos, also the estimator $\fNLest$ inherits this chaotic structure and can be expressed as an element in the third Wiener chaos. This observation is crucial because it allows us to apply the Fourth Moment Theorem to study its asymptotic behavior. \\ 
In this section, we establish a quantitative central limit theorem for $\fNLest$ in total variation distance. The total variation distance between two probability measures $P$ and $Q$ on a measurable space $\Omega, \mathcal{F}$ is defined as
\begin{equation*}
\dW\left(P,Q\right) = \underset{A \in \mathcal{F} }{\sup} \left \vert P(A) - Q(A) \right \vert. 
	\end{equation*}
 In particular, we will use the celebrated results known as the ``fourth moment theorem" (see, for example, \cite[Theorem 5.2.6]{noupebook}). If F belongs to the $q$-th Wiener chaos, then 
 \begin{equation*} 
 	\dW\left(F,Z\right) \leq 2\sqrt{\frac{q-1}{3q}\frac{\cumq{F}}{\left(\Var{F}\right)^2}},
 \end{equation*}
 where $Z$ is a standard normal random variable.\\
In light of Remark \ref{rem:chaos}, since $\Bispest$ belongs to the third Wiener chaos (i.e., $q=3$), any linear combination of such terms also lies in the third chaos. This leads to the following bound for the centered estimator $\fNLest-\fNL$:
%\begin{theorem}\label{th:main}
%$\fNLest$ converges in distribution to $Z$ with a rate given by the following bound
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:mainconvergence}
\dW\left( \fNLest - \fNL, Z \right) \leq  
\frac{2\sqrt{2}}{3}\sqrt{\frac{\cumq{\fNLest} }{\left(\Var{\fNLest}\right)^2 }},
\end{equation*}
where $Z\sim\mathcal{N}\left( 0,1\right)$. 
%\end{theorem}
\subsection{Some auxiliary results}
We begin by stating an a lemma that will play a central role in understanding the behavior of our estimator, and in making its dependence on the maximum angular resolution $L$ explicit. Let preliminarily define the following subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intdomain}
	\Ar = \lbrace \left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right) \in \mathbb{R}^3, r\leq x_1<x_2<x_3 \leq 1; x_2-x_1 \leq x_3 \leq x_1+x_2 \rbrace,
\end{equation}
This set consists of all triples  $\left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right)$ with strictly increasing entries in $\left(r,1\right)$ such that they can be read as the side lengths of a triangle. The parameter $r \in (0,1/2)$ acts as a lower cutoff, ensuring none of the sides are too small. 
We will use also  $\Delta: \mathbb{R}^3 \mapsto \mathbb{R}$, given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:delta}
	\Delta\left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right)=\left(x_1+x_2+x_3\right)\left(x_1+x_2-x_3\right)\left(x_1-x_2+x_3\right)\left(-x_1+x_2+x_3\right),
\end{equation}
which is striclty positive for all real, non-negative side lengths $\left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right)$ satisfying the triangle inequalities — meaning the three numbers can form the sides of a non-degenerate triangle. Also, it follows form the Heron's formula that $\Delta\left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right) = 16T$, where $T$  is the area of the triangle with side lengths $\left(x_1,x_2,x_3\right)$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:integral}
	For $(a,b,c,d) \in \mathbb{R}$, and $r \in \left(0,1/2\right)$, let $D_r(a,b,c,d)$ be given by 
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:genint}
		D_r(a,b,c,d) = \int_{\Ar} \frac{x_1^a x_2^bx_3^d}{\Delta^d(x_1,x_2,x_3)} \diff x_1 \diff x_2 \diff x_3,
	\end{equation}
	where $\Ar$ and $\Delta:\mathbb{R}^3\mapsto \mathbb{R}$ are given by \eqref{eq:intdomain} and \eqref{eq:delta} respectively. Let $\lambda=a+b+c-4d+3$. Then it holds that 
	\begin{align}\label{eq:Gfinale}
		D_r(a,b,c,d) = \begin{cases}
			\frac{1}{\lambda} \int_{\Omega_r} \frac{u^av^b}{Q^d(u,v)}\left(1-\left(\frac{r}{u}\right)^\lambda\right) 	\diff u \diff v& \text{ if }\lambda \neq 0		 \\
		\int_{\Omega_r} \frac{u^av^b}{Q^d(u,v)}\log\left(\frac{r}{u}\right) \diff u \diff v	& \text{ if }\lambda \neq 0		
			\end{cases},
	\end{align}
where $Q: \mathbb{R}^\mapsto \mathbb{R}$ is given by 
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:Q}
	Q\left(u,v\right)=\Delta\left(u,v,1\right)= \left(u+v+1\right) \left(u+v-1\right) \left(u-v+1\right) \left(-u+v+1\right),
\end{equation*}
and the integration domain is defined by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:omegadomain}
	\begin{split}
&\Omega_r=\Omega_{1,r} \cup \Omega_{2,r}; \\ 
& \Omega_{1,r} =\lbrace (u,v): r<u<\frac{1}{2}, 1-u<v<1\rbrace;\\
&  \Omega_{2,r} =\lbrace (u,v): \frac{1}{2}<u<1, u<v<1\rbrace.
\end{split}
\end{equation} 
\end{lemma}

