The following proposition shows that $\fNLest$ is unbiased, and provides an upper bound for the fourth cumulant and an integral approximation for the variance of the $\fNL$ estimator; both results will be instrumental in establishing the main quantitative limit theorem presented in this work.
\begin{proposition}\label{lemmaest}
	Let $\widehat{f}_{NL}$ be given by (\ref{eq:estimatordef}). Under the previous assumptions, for $\alpha >4$, 
	it holds that 
	\begin{align*}
		&\underset{L \rightarrow \infty }{\lim}  \Ex{ \fNLest}=\fNL ;\\
		&\underset{L \rightarrow \infty }{\lim} L^{4-\alpha} \Var{ \fNLest} =\sigma_{\fNL}^2;\label{eq:varf}\\
		&0<\underset{L \rightarrow \infty }{\lim} L^{2\alpha-12} \cumq{\fNLest} \leq K_{\fNL} ,
	\end{align*}
	where 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{split}
		&	\sigma_{\fNL}^2 = \frac{1}{C_{\eta^2}I_{\eta^2;r} \left(\alpha\right)};\\ 
		&K_{\fNL}=\frac{C_{\kappa}I_{\kappa;r}\left(\alpha\right)}{C_{\eta^2}^4 I^4_{\eta;r}}.
		\end{split}
	\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}

We are now in a position to present our main result: the following theorem establishes a quantitative central limit theorem for the nonlinearity parameter estimator, capturing its asymptotic Gaussian behavior with an explicit rate of convergence. n order to establish the central limit theorem, it is sufficient to perform a detailed analysis of fourth-order cumulants, leveraging the fact that our statistic belongs to the third-order Wiener chaos with respect to a Gaussian white noise random measure. This approach allows us to apply the fourth cumulant criterion developed by \cite{nourdinpeccati} to rigorously quantify convergence to normality.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
Under the notation established above, it holds that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mainth}
	\dW\left( \fNLest - \fNL, Z \right) \leq 
	\CW L^{-2},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation*}
\CW=\frac{4}{\sqrt{3}}\frac{I^{\frac{1}{2}}_{\kappa;r}\left(\alpha\right)}{I_{\eta^2;r}\left(\alpha\right)}. 
\end{equation*}
Also, as $L\rightarrow \infty$, $\fNLest-\fNL$ converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable, that is,
\begin{equation*}
	\fNLest-\fNL \xrightarrow{d} \mathcal{N}(0,1)
\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{remark}[Some heuristics on the convergence rate]
	\leavevmode
\begin{enumerate}
\item The key to understanding the speed $L^{-2}$ of our total variation bound concerns how the estimator improves as the number of available bispectrum modes grows. More precisely, recall that the cardinality of elements in $\LambdaL$, given by \eqref{eq:LambdaL}, the set of all admissible multipole‐triplets satisfying the triangle and parity constraints is propositional to $\NL=O\left(L^3\right)$. Indeed, as stated in Remark \ref{rem:Lambda}, each of the multipoles $\ell_1,$ $\ell_2$, and $\ell_3$ ranges over an 
$O\left(L\right)$ window (up to constant factors), and the triangle inequalities \eqref{eq:ell} cut out only an $O\left(1\right)$-fraction of the full set. We can rewrite the bound in Equation \eqref{eq:mainth} in Theorem \ref{thm:main}, we may re‐express that rate in terms of $\NL$ as 
\begin{equation*}
	\dW\left( \fNLest - \fNL, Z \right) \leq O\left(\NL^{-\frac{2}{3}}\right),
\end{equation*}
When comparing this to a classical CLT rate $O\left(\NL^{-\frac{1}{2}}\right)$, here we see a strictly faster decay exponent, since $2/3>1/2$. This super‐parametric exponent is a direct consequence of the cubic growth of admissible multipole‐triplets combined with the bispectral weighting structure.
\item A similar result concerning objects belonging to the third Wiener chaos can be derived from a canonical example studied by \cite[Section 4]{npopt}, in the spirit of the Breuer–Major theorem. The example considers the normalized sum $F_N = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{k=1}^{N}H_{3}\left( X_k\right)$, where $H_3$ is the third Hermite polynomial and $\lbrace X_k : k=1,\ldots,N\rbrace$ is a stationary Gaussian sequence with covariance $\rho{j} = \left \vert j \right \vert ^{-\beta}$. This sequence lives in the third Wiener chaos, and the total variation distance between $F_N$ and $Z\sim \mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$ converges at the rate $O\left(N^{1-4\beta}\right)$. This provides an optimal benchmark for third-chaos bispectrum-type estimators. Also, for the critical decay rate $\beta=\frac{2}{3}$, the convergence rate become $O\left(N^{-\frac{2}{3}}\right)$, as in our case.
\item It is possible to draw an analogy with the classic Gauss circle problem. Let
$\nu_{R} = \operatorname{card}\lbrace\left(j,k\right)\in \mathbb{Z}^2 : j^2+k^2=R^2\rbrace$ be the number of integer lattice points in the disk of radius 
$R$. According for example to \cite{hardy79}, it is possible to show that
$$\nu(R)=\pi R^2 + \epsilon(R),$$ 
where $\epsilon(R)$ is the error term, of order $O(R^2/3)$. The relative error in approximating the disk’s area $\pi R^2$ by lattice points is given by
$$ 
\frac{\epsilon(R)}{\nu(R)} = \left(R^{-\frac{4}{3}}\right) =\left(N^{-\frac{2}{3}}\right).
$$
In our estimator, we sum over indices in $\LambdaL$, which form a $3$-dimensional simplex of integer points in $\mathbb{N}^3$, and approximate this discrete sum by an integral of a third‐Wiener‐chaos functional over the corresponding continuous region. Gauss’s circle problem makes the exact same kind of approximation in two dimensions—counting lattice points inside a disk by its area $\pi R^2$, with the error coming entirely from points near the circular boundary. In both cases the dominant contribution to the error comes from the $d-1$-dimensional boundary (here 
$d=3$ for the tetrahedron, there $d=2$ for the circle), which forces a convergence rate of order $N^{-\frac{d}{d-1}}$. 
Geometrically, whenever one approximates a $d$-dimensional volume by discrete sampling, the surface–to–volume ratio governs the error, yielding this universal  $d/(d-1)$ exponent. In our case, looking at a relative error in total variation distance for $\fNLest$ versus the relative error in Gauss’s problem, the$d/(d-1)$ exponent falls out of exactly the same boundary-vs-volume balance. This boundary‐dominated phenomenon recurs throughout discrepancy theory, lattice‐point enumeration, and empirical process theory (see, e.g., \cite{niederreiter}).
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}


