\section{Introduction}

Large Language Models (LLMs) are transformer-based models \cite{vaswani2017attention} designed to generate text, that users interact with using natural language. These models are pre-trained on a large quantity of data covering a variety of domains and linguistic styles \cite{achiam2023gpt}. Training data is usually imported from web pages, newspaper articles, books, documentation or literature \cite{radford2018improving} \cite{radford2019language} \cite{brown2020language}. 
Llama3 \cite{dubey2024llama} was trained on 15 trillion \( (1.5 \times 10^{13}) \) tokens. However, training on such large datasets requires significant computational resources, raising concerns about time, cost, and energy consumption \cite{singh2024survey}.

Training these models on large code datasets enables them to assist users and developers, showing strong potential for code generation. A notable example is \emph{GitHub Copilot} \cite{peng2023impact}, an assistant designed to provide suggestions, error corrections, and autocompletions of code. It is powered by OpenAI’s Codex model \cite{chen2021evaluating}, a general purpose programming model. Copilot analyzes the context in which code is written, and generates contextually relevant suggestions in real-time, potentially accelerating the coding process.

However, one major problem with LLMs is when the model generates incorrect or unfounded information. This phenomenon is called hallucination \cite{huang2023survey}. It is a particularly concerning problem in specialized or underrepresented domains in the training dataset, like Reservoir Computing \cite{jaeger2001echo}. As such, even if ChatGPT-4o \cite{hurst2024gpt} knows what a reservoir is, it could first confuse it with a water reservoir. Even when clarified within the context of machine learning, it will not always give an accurate answer, and it could provide answers that seems plausible but are actually incorrect.

Reservoir Computing models are not mainstream, despite their performance in different tasks, particularly those involving small sequential datasets or challenging corpora with complex non-linear variable interactions such as COVID hospitalization predictions \cite{ferte:hal-04693930}. This under-representation can make it difficult for users, especially those new to the field, to fully appreciate and harness the potential of Reservoir Computing, and in fact to efficiently parametrize reservoir models.

\emph{ReservoirPy}~\cite{trouvain2020reservoirpy,trouvain2022create,trouvainHALjmlr} %,} 
is a Python library that allows users to easily create and manipulate Reservoir Computing models. But, ReservoirPy users can face challenges when trying to configure reservoir models to understand the underlying intuitions and correctly parametrize the models. Moreover, reading the full documentation can be time-consuming, especially for new users. An interactive assistant may offer a more intuitive and accessible alternative to static documentation, helping users efficiently locate specific features without getting lost in the API.
More generally, Reservoir Computing needs some expertise to properly set up a  model. For instance, hyperparameter tuning could be a challenge for some tasks\footnote{For instance, there is the misconception that values above 1 should not be explored for the \emph{spectral radius} because of theoretical insights that are not well understood on the echo state property.}, and ReservoirPy propose hyperparameter tutorials~\cite{hinaut2021hype}. 

In the following, we propose various versions of an improved LLM augmented with code and documents. Then, we compare its performance with its base model and other mainstream models. Finally, we discuss the strengths and limitations of this approach.












\section{Methods}

\subsection{Embeddings and Simple ChatBot}

ReservoirChat development began with a simple chatbot designed to answer 245 predefined questions (see GitHub repo), with their corresponding answers. The goal was to determine if a simple application, without the involvement of text generator (LLM decoder), could be sufficient. The entire database was converted into vectors using text embeddings, in our case according to the \textit{nomic-embed-text-v1.5} \cite{nussbaum2024nomic} model (LLM encoder) from Nomic-AI with \emph{LM Studio}. Each user-submitted question was vectorized and compared with the database through a cosine similarity process, in order to make correlations between the questions and the database. Based on the closest match, a response would be selected and delivered to the user. This basic approach becomes more effective as the database grows.

When a user submitted a query, the chatbot retrieved the top five most similar embeddings based on cosine similarity between the query embedding and the database of prepared questions. 

The system's limitations became apparent when users asked questions that were not explicitly covered in the database. This issue was exacerbated when handling coding-related questions, where even a slight variation in the code or phrasing of the question could result in the chatbot failing to find a suitable match. This revealed the need for a more sophisticated system capable of handling a wider range of queries, including those that required deeper contextual understanding or more complex reasoning challenges. This preliminary model served as a baseline, limited to simple, predictable questions.














\subsection{Retrieval-Augmented Generation and ReservoirRag}

A more elaborate approach consists in enhancing an LLM with a database. As stated previously, hallucination occurs when LLMs generate incorrect, but sometimes plausible answers, due to a lack of factual basis. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) \cite{NEURIPS2020_6b493230} \cite{gao2023retrieval} can mitigate this issue without requiring additional model training by dynamically retrieving relevant information from external documentation sources before generating a response, provided the retrieved data has been properly validated. However, if incorrect sources are introduced, RAG will propagate these errors, hence making careful conservation of the knowledge base essential. Assuming the database remains up-to-date, RAG can be easily adjusted with new information at a significantly lower computational cost compared to retraining an entire model. Since RAG influences the accuracy and reliability of responses, selecting an appropriate LLM remains a critical decision, as some models offer superior multilingual capabilities while others excel in coding assistance.

RAG requires the database to be prepared and cut into chunks. These chunks of text provide a basis for generating responses, treated as text embeddings, and correlated according to the previous model. Chunk size is defined by the user depending on the context and parameters. It is possible, for example, to choose segments of 10 lines, entire paragraphs, or even complete portions of code. In addition, it is sometimes appropriate to overlap chunks to maintain context coherence.

Thus, the chatbot evolved into ReservoirRAG, when we decided to connect Codestral-22B \cite{mistral2024codestral} to a RAG. Codestral is a multilingual model developed by Mistral, known for its effective handling of coding queries. The base data consists of documents, including the ReservoirPy documentation, relevant papers about Reservoir Computing, and code samples. After processing, embeddings are generated from these documents and stored in a CSV file alongside their content. These precomputed embeddings are then loaded using the \emph{pandas} library and organized into a DataFrame. Some additional documents were processed in the same manner, but reserved as external data for RAG.

Again, embedding relevance was based on the same test as before, with a 0.75 correlation threshold. This implementation ensures that only the most relevant data is considered, enhancing response accuracy. Once the relevant documents are identified, they are incorporated into the LLM’s response generation process as part of the contextual information, alongside the conversation history, enabling the model to retain memory of previous queries and responses. For queries related to coding, the additional custom document codes.md, is added to the database, containing various code snippets and samples from the ReservoirPy GitHub. This feature is particularly important for coding-related queries, as even minor errors in generated code could lead to incorrect answers.








\subsection{Knowledge graph, GraphRAG and ReservoirChat}

A Knowledge Graph (Figure \ref{fig-clustered-graph-reservoirchat}) \cite{chen2020knowledge} is a structured representation of a dataset that organizes information into entities and their relationships, represented respectively as nodes and edges. Knowledge graphs allow LLMs to associate different concepts in a more qualitative manner, improving the contextual and semantic accuracy of answers. Unlike simply implementing RAG, this method requires preprocessing the documents during the initial creation of a graph, which can become time and resource intensive for a complete and thorough result. It is nevertheless lighter than retraining the entire model.

