The GraphRAG \cite{edgetrinh2024graphrag} method, developed by Microsoft, stands out for its ability to handle a large and complex volume of data, in the form of corpora, such as scientific papers. Where traditional RAG shines when it comes to specific questions, GraphRAG is more versatile, and if effective at answering both specific questions, and broader queries about the general theme of a corpus.

According to the methodology (Figure \ref{fig-pipeline}) described by \cite{edgetrinh2024graphrag}, the knowledge graph is constructed starting from a database of documents, which are cut into chunks as detailed before. Using LLM-driven prompts, the system identifies named entities and the relationships between them. This is repeated in several rounds to ensure that all relevant entities are captured. Each individual element is summarized according to a domain-tailored summarizing process. Then, the system builds an undirected graph in which each node represents an entity, and each edge captures the relationship between two entities. The edges are weighted based on the frequency and strength of the relationships. Algorithms like Leiden \cite{Traag_2019} are used to partition the newly created graph, detecting clusters of closely related nodes within the graph. These clusters, also known as communities, reflect topics or themes that are highly interconnected (Figure \ref{fig-clustered-graph-reservoirchat-zoom}). A summary is later generated for each community, helping to create partial answers to user queries by drawing from multiple related entities. These partial or local answers may be relevant on their own, or summarized even further into a so-called global answer. In the case of ReservoirChat and its final version, it was more appropriate to mainly utilize local answers, as they provide more focused and detailed information than a global response. While more comprehensive, global responses tend to be less precise, which is intuitively less desirable for the scientific and educational objectives of ReservoirChat.

