\subsection{Evaluation method}

Response quality in natural language is complex to evaluate, hence the need to consider a systematic approach. Human evaluation is not preferred, as it would both be highly time-consuming and require expertise, which is costly, in addition to being vulnerable to subjectivity and cognitive bias. LLM-based evaluation was also excluded, as there are currently no LLM specifically trained about ReservoirPy and Reservoir Computing, therefore leading to potential imprecision and unreliability.

Benchmarking was the chosen method, being the most objective one. A set of questions, written by a Reservoir Computing expert, were given to the LLM. The model was tasked with selecting the correct answer from 4 suggestions. 

In the following experiment, we compared every model of ReservoirChat we created with ChatGPT-4o, Llama3, Codestral and NotebookLM \cite{google2024notebooklm} (based on Gemini 1.5 \cite{team2024gemini}). Only ReservoirChat and NotebookLM were fed with documents about Reservoir Computing and ReservoirPy. ChatGPT-4o, Llama3 and Codestral relied only on their own knowledge and training methods to answer. We tried different sets of documents for NotebookLM. NotebookLM$_{Core}$ includes the same documents as ReservoirChat$_{Basic}$ except the documentation. NotebookLM$_{Other}$ includes every document not included in the core version, this was added to test the influence of the core model compared to the other model. The NotebookLM$_{Big}$ represents all the documents like ReservoirChat$_{Big}$. For every model a temperature of 0.1 was used to minimize stochastic effects, as preliminary tests showed it was a good compromise and close to a temperature of 0 \footnote{Temperature in an LLM controls freedom in responses, on a scale from 0 to 1, 0 resulting in more deterministic answers with no variation. This setting influences the model's creativity in generating answers.} which is often preferred \cite{renze2024effect}. However, ChatGPT-4o  does not offer the possibility to change temperature, nor does NotebookLM. 

The benchmark consisted of 20 knowledge-based questions and 14 coding-related questions, including 8 debugging tasks. To minimize bias, each question was asked independently, without prior context. A standardized prompt designed to reduce any disparity was provided to ensure fair comparison across all models : 

\blockquote{You will be given a question with four answer options labeled A, B, C, and D. Please respond using the library ReservoirPy with only the letter (A, B, C, or D) that is the correct answer.}

Each question were asked 3 times, therefore a score of 0, 0.33, 0.66, or 1 respectively indicates that the model gave the correct answer zero, once, twice or three times out of three attempts. The total score of a model is the addition of the individual score for each question, respectively on a scale from 0 to 20 for the first benchmark, then from 0 to 14 for the second, with 0 indicating the lowest possible performance.


















