In this section, we aim to compare ReservoirChat, which is based on the Codestral architecture, to various classical models such as LLaMA3 and GPT-4o, as well as other models capable of learning from external databases, such as NotebookLM. Specifically, the results focus on evaluating two important aspects: knowledge-based questions related to Reservoir Computing concepts and coding/debugging tasks involving the ReservoirPy library. We also examine how incorporating external resources, such as scientific papers and documentation, impacts the performance of several models. The evaluation uses a custom-made benchmark developed to assess the accuracy and consistency of responses in the context of Reservoir Computing (see Methods). 

\subsection{Analysis of Knowledge-Based Performance}

Regarding knowledge questions (Figure \ref{fig-result-knowledge}), Llama3 and Codestral have comparable score of 12.66 and 11, respectively. All ReservoirChat models output higher scores than the default Codestral, increasing with each addition of documents from a score of 15 for the Basic model, up to a score of 18.66 with the ReservoirChat$_{Big}$. Performance improves proportionally with the number of documents added to ReservoirChat’s database. Further improvements could be achieved by adding more documents and fine-tuning the model. Also ChatGPT-4o and all NotebookLM versions obtain a score of 20. This perfect score highlights a superior ability to answer knowledge-based questions compared to other models.

\subsection{Analysis of Code and Debug Performance}

On the other hand, code and debug questions (Figure \ref{fig-result-code}) have an overall much lower performance for all models, with none having or approaching a perfect score. ChatGPT-4o performed moderately at 7. NotebookLM ranges from 7.66 to 8.66. It is interesting to note that the Core version, trained on resolved issues, has a worse score than the Other version which includes ReservoirPy documentation and scientific papers about reservoirs and the library. The difference, slightly in favor of NotebookLM$_{Other}$, equals to 8.33 - 7.66 = 0.67, or 4.8\%, could indicate that an LLM trained on a whole language documentation is more reliable in coding assistance than one only trained on resolved issues. NotebookLM$_{Big}$ outputs a similar score of 8.66. 
These results may be biased, as in real-world scenarios, developers often face novel issues not covered by existing resolved cases, potentially lowering NotebookLM$_{Core}$'s performance. 

Llama3 yields better results than Codestral, with a score of 6 against 5. Given that Codestral is known for its reliability in coding assistance, this result surprising, and suggest that as ReservoirPy is a recent library, the model may not have been trained on a lot of data apart from the documentation in itself. Codestral's usual reliability must then be founded on training with larger or different type of dataset. This discrepancy may also be due to how the questions were posed, or the fact that Codestral’s training corpus may focus less on English and more on other languages.

ReservoirChat models also outperform Codestral, obtaining a score of 9.66 for the Basic and Little Models, 7 for Medium, and 8.66 for Big. These results may be indicative of the influence of other non-code related documents on the Knowledge Graph. The larger the database, the more complex and numerous the connections between entities are, potentially affecting some answers. Also, Medium having the lowest score for coding questions, out of all the ReservoirChat versions tested, could indicate that there is a limit up to which more data confuses the model but there is not enough data to adjust its own performance yet.

Finally, it is interesting to note when asked three times, ChatGPT-4o's provided different answers to 50\% of the coding questions, suggesting that it has a high temperature. In comparison, when asked three times, ReservoirChat$_{Big}$ provided different answers to only 21.4\% of the coding questions. Also, questions were arranged into categories and levels of difficulty, but no evident differences were observed.

