For each model, we respectively concatenated the 3 answers they gave for the knowledge-related and the code-related questions into two vectors, and we computed the Pearson correlation between all of them. We can see those results in the Figure \ref{fig-correlation-knowledge} and \ref{fig-correlation-code} that respectively show the knowledge-related and code-related correlation matrices. You can find the correlation matrix on the whole questions in the Appendix (available on our repository).

As we can expect, models that perform well are correlated together, because there is only one good answer per question. So, for the knowledge part where lots of models obtained good results, it is not a surprise to see that a lot of models are well correlated together, as we can see with GPT-4o, and all NotebookLM versions which have a correlation score of 1, as they all answered perfectly on all the knowledge questions. Conversely, the less a model performs, the less it will be correlated with the others, as we can see with Codestral. One exception is Llama3, which does not perform well on the knowledge related questions, but is a bit correlated with other high-performing models.

On the other hand, when we look at the code-related matrix correlation, we can observe two major groups of models : those related to NotebookLM and those related to ReservoirChat. In both groups, models are well correlated together, which is expected, because they respectively derive from the same base. We can also observe this with Codestral that is a bit correlated to ReservoirChat but not at all to other models. In fact, Codestral appears a bit isolated, with correlation score close to 0 with every model except ReservoirChat and Llama3. This might be due to the fact that Codestral is the only model specifically trained for coding tasks. As an unexpected results, we also observe a correlation between Llama3 and NotebookLM Other which might be due to the documents there were fed with, and the near-zero correlation between ReservoirChat${Medium}$ and Codestral. 

\subsection{Summary of Findings and Future Directions}

These results are encouraging for RAG based knowledge graph methods. Despite the base model's limitations, our approach improves its performance in domain-specific tasks, particularly in Reservoir Computing. 



\section{User data collection with Big ReservoirChat}

Over three months, 130 users interacted with ReservoirChat${Big}$. Some questions were directly related to the ReservoirPy library, while others required broader coding knowledge beyond the preloaded database, relying on Codestral’s base model. 
On average, people asked between two to five questions, indicating that while the tool raises curiosity, it’s not yet fully utilized as an assistant.

We analyzed data collected to get inspiration on which Q\&A we could put in the benchmark. In the following section, we present a selection of two representative user questions and the corresponding responses given by ReservoirChat$_{Big}$ between August and November.

\subsection{Example of knowledge question}



\noindent\textbf{User:}\\
How can a random matrix make such results ? Why does it work ? How can it store such an amount of information ?

\noindent\textbf{ReservoirChat:}\\
A random matrix can make such results because it is a matrix that is generated using random values. This randomness can lead to the creation of complex patterns and structures that can capture and store a significant amount of information.

\noindent
The ability of a random matrix to store information is a result of its dimensionality and the distribution of its values. A high-dimensional random matrix can potentially have a large number of unique configurations, which can be used to represent a wide range of data. The distribution of values in the matrix also plays a role, as certain distributions can lead to more efficient packing of information.

\noindent
However, it's important to note that the effectiveness of a random matrix in storing information can vary depending on the specific characteristics of the data and the task at hand. While random matrices can be powerful tools, they may not always be the best choice for every situation.


