\section{Discussion}

To summarize, there is a clear need for a tool capable of assisting users working with Reservoir Computing, an underrepresented technology in the literature. While traditional LLMs can partially fulfill this task, they tend to hallucinate and provide incorrect results on their own, especially in specialized areas such as Reservoir Computing. Moreover, even though the ReservoirPy library is acknowledged by the community, there is not as much code available online as for other mainstream libraries such as scikit-learn. Thus, general LLMs do not have enough examples to train on for domain-specific libraries.

We had to make choices during tool development, beginning with Microsoft's GraphRAG library, which was not originally designed to be used with LLMs other than ChatGPT. As a result, we had to identify LLMs and embeddings that match our project. This included requirements such as code generation, answering questions about RCs, available computational power, and support for European languages. 
In this context, Codestral emerged as a suitable choice \cite{jiang2023mistral} \cite{jiang2024mixtral}.
However, running these models locally requires high computing power, and even though Codestral can be considered as a small model with its 22 billions parameters, it still required the use of a GPU (NVIDIA V100 16Go) \cite{bendi2024deploying}. 

ReservoirChat has shown better results on the benchmark in both knowledge-based and coding tasks compared to Codestral, the model on which it is based. Moreover, a larger database intuitively tends to provide an acceptable trade-off between reliability in answering knowledge-based and code-related questions. Additionally, methods implemented in ReservoirChat$_{Big}$ do not produce aberrant ouputs, nor responses entirely different from those of other models. Finally, ReservoirChat$_{Big}$ fulfills its objective, by being able to relevantly answer both knowledge and coding questions towards Reservoir Computing and ReservoirPy, with higher reliability than other tested commonly used LLMs.

It is important to note that GraphRAG was originally developed to extract concepts and relationships from narrative texts. However, the concepts involved in the challenges we encountered, such as code related tasks or technical knowledge in Reservoir Computing, are significantly different and may require prompts tailored to these domains, rather than those originally designed for GraphRAG. A potential improvement could be to refine GraphRAG's prompts to better extract the concepts and relationships relevant to our areas of interest. 

This approach could serve as a model for making technical documentation more interactive.
It could represents a significant advancement in how users engage with specialized libraries and apply them in their own projects. With further refinements, ReservoirChat could become a reliable and practical solution for every Reservoir Computing developer. Fine-tuning hyperparameters and system settings could help optimize response time and quality. Furthermore, careful selection of the LLM remains a critical component of system performance.

Additional testing should also be considered to ensure safety, no inappropriate response should be generated even in unanticipated use cases. Data fairness should be verified, as always, to avoid any misuse of intellectual property, as it is an important concern for LLM pretraining. The knowledge graph method could also be compared to alternative approaches in terms of answer interpretability, server resource requirements, and cost of minimal configuration for acceptable user experience.

The ReservoirChat tool, based on a GraphRAG approach combined with a specialized knowledge base, addresses the practical needs of the Reservoir Computing community. It currently simplifies the learning and application of libraries like ReservoirPy, notably by assisting with debugging and suggesting relevant hyperparameters (e.g., spectral radius, leak rate). For instance, a Phd student tackling their first Echo State Network project can turn to ReservoirChat as a technical partner for configuring and optimizing their network, while an engineer seeking to deploy Reservoir Computing modules in a production environment can quickly resolve compatibility and performance issues.

Moving forward, continuously adding new scientific publications to the knowledge graph will allow ReservoirChat to stay aligned with advances in the field (e.g., hybrid architectures, photonic or spintronic physical reservoirs, and Deep RC). This would enable researchers to interactively consult recent papers and code prototypes to identify promising configurations more efficiently. Moreover, the graph-based approach offers improved traceability and interpretability: by retracing inference paths, users can not only obtain more reliable responses but also deepen their understanding of key Reservoir Computing concepts. These developments position ReservoirChat as a versatile tool that supports research, fosters innovation, and serves as an interactive documentation and educational resource for the Reservoir Computing community.


