\subsection*{\textbf{Knowledge questions (Beginner)}}
%\\
%\subsubsection*{\textbf{Beginner}}

\subsection*{1. What is an echo state network?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. A place where anything is kept in store.
  \item B. A recurrent neural network in which usually only the output neurons are trained.
  \item C.  A black box component that receives an input signal to be read out and mapped by another process.
  \item D. A large natural or artificial lake used as a source of water supply.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{2. What is Reservoir Computing?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. A machine learning paradigm for timeseries.
  \item B. The construction of a computer model of a petroleum reservoir.
  \item C. A branch of fluid dynamics that studies the design of tank trucks to reduce rollover accidents.
  \item D. It is only a computational framework in which all the computations are done by a pool of water.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3. What is call the readout? (in the context of Reservoir Computing)}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. A linear regression that models the Markovian process of a support vector machine.
  \item B. An efficient way for input processing in order to read only part of the inputs.
  \item C. A black box component that receives an input signal to be read out and mapped back to the input for predictive coding.
  \item D. A trainable layer that extracts the dynamics of a reservoir to produce some desired outputs.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{4. On which task is Reservoir Computing known to compete with the state-of-the-art?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Video segmentation.
  \item B. Natural language processing.
  \item C. Image recognition.
  \item D. Chaotic timeseries prediction.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{5. On which task is it challenging to apply Reservoir Computing compared to other state-of-the-art methods?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. A task with small univariate dataset.
  \item B. Video segmentation.
  \item C. Chaotic timeseries prediction.
  \item D. Classification of timeseries with few classes.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{6. Approximately how many neurons are used inside an echo state network?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Around 1 thousand neurons.
  \item B. Around 100 thousand neurons.
  \item C. Around 10 neurons.
  \item D. Around 1 million neurons.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

\subsection*{7. What is the purpose of using ridge regression instead of linear regression for the readout?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Reduce the computational cost.
  \item B. Improve numerical stability.
  \item C. Improve explainability of the model.
  \item D. Avoid the exploding/vanishing gradient problem.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{8. How are the weights most often initialized in an echo state network?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. They can be randomly initialized and then scaled to have a specific spectral radius.
  \item B. They are tuned according to an autocorrelation Hebbian learning rule.
  \item C. Trained using a linear regression.
  \item D. Each neuron is connected to only one input.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

