\subsection*{\textbf{Knowledge questions (Intermediate)}}

\subsection*{9. What is the difference between ‘echo state network’ and ‘Reservoir Computing’?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Reservoir Computing is a type of recurrent neural network model based on the philosophy of the echo state network.
  \item B. There is no difference, we can use the terms Reservoir Computing and echo state network indistinctly.
  \item C. An echo state network is a type of recurrent neural network model based on the Reservoir Computing paradigm.
  \item D. In Reservoir Computing, the reservoir part is a physical system, in echo state networks, the reservoir part is a recurrent neural network.
\end{itemize}

Correct: C

\subsection*{10. Are there other forms of Reservoir Computing than echo state networks?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. No, an echo state network is not even a form of Reservoir Computing.
  \item B. Yes, any random kernel method, even those who don't apply to timeseries, are considered to be a form of Reservoir Computing.
  \item C. No, Reservoir Computing only refers to the philosophy behind echo state networks.
  \item D. Yes, there are for instance physical reservoirs or liquid state machines, in which the reservoir is a spiking neural network.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{11. What is a liquid state machine?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. An architecture in which the reservoir part is a pool of spiking neurons.
  \item B. A physical reservoir in which the reservoir is a reservoir of liquid, usually water.
  \item C. Liquid state machine and Reservoir Computing designate the same concept.
  \item D. A computational model of the hippocampus using the Reservoir Computing paradigm.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

\subsection*{12. Why is it called ‘computing at the edge of chaos’?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Because it is common to add noise (and thus chaos) to the reservoir in order to stabilize its activity.
  \item B. Because Reservoir Computing works best for chaotic timeseries forecasting.
  \item C. Because Reservoir Computing often works best when the dynamics of the reservoir are approaching chaotic dynamics, but are not chaotic.
  \item D. It requires computers to be physically placed at the edge of a chaotic environment, such as a volcano or a kindergarten.
\end{itemize}

Correct: C

\subsection*{13. What is the ‘echo state property’?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. The echo state property allows the reservoir to operate with chaotic behavior to enhance computational power.
  \item B. The ability to memorize past activity and cycle over it (the echo).
  \item C. The ability to perfectly reconstruct any input signal from the reservoir activity.
  \item D. The influence of initial states on the reservoir dynamics decays over time, ensuring that the current state primarily reflects recent inputs.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{14. What are some of the most important hyper-parameters?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. The spectral radius of the recurrent weight matrix, the leak rate, the input scaling.
  \item B. The spectral radius of the recurrent weight matrix, the reservoir connectivity, the weight distribution.
  \item C. The spectral radius of the recurrent weight matrix, the reservoir connectivity, the encoding of the input.
  \item D. The activation function, the reservoir connectivity, the regularization parameter.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

