\subsection*{\textbf{Code questions (not debug)}}

\subsection*{1. I want to train my echo state network on multiple timeseries that have different lengths. I have seen in the documentation that you can put a 3D numpy array with shape (timeseries, timesteps, dimensions), but it wouldn't work in my case as the timeseries have different lengths.}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. There is no way to do that in ReservoirPy as it is most probably not a good idea to train a model with different lengths and it would induce unexpected results.
  \item B. You can pass a list of 2D numpy arrays that represents timeseries. As lists can contain numpy arrays of different shapes, you can specify timeseries with different lengths.
  \item C. You would have to pad every timeseries with zeros and then concatenate them.
  \item D. As NumPy doesn't support sparse arrays, it is best to use xarray for this use-case.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{2. Make me a reservoir, with 1000 neurons, and with a uniform distribution of weights, and a sparsity of 95\%.}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy as rpy
reservoir = rpy.nodes.Reservoir(neurons=1_000, connectivity=0.05, weights="uniform")
\end{lstlisting}
  \item B. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy as rpy
reservoir = rpy.nodes.Reservoir(units=1_000, sparsity=0.95, W=rpy.mat_gen.uniform)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item C. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy as rpy
reservoir = rpy.Reservoir(units=1_000, rc_connectivity=0.05, distr="uniform")
\end{lstlisting}
  \item D. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy as rpy
reservoir = rpy.nodes.Reservoir(units=1_000, rc_connectivity=0.05, W=rpy.mat_gen.uniform)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{3. Create a model in which there are several reservoirs connected in a chain, and a readout at the end.}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge
model = [Reservoir(100, name="1"), Reservoir(100, name="2"), 
         Reservoir(100, name="3"), Reservoir(100, name="4"), 
         Reservoir(100, name="5"), Ridge(ridge=1e-5)]
\end{lstlisting}
  \item B. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge
model = Reservoir(100, name="1") >> Reservoir(100, name="2") 
        >> Reservoir(100, name="3") >> Reservoir(100, name="4") 
        >> Reservoir(100, name="5") >> Ridge(ridge=1e-5)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item C. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge
model = Reservoir(100) > Reservoir(100) > Reservoir(100) 
        > Reservoir(100) > Reservoir(100) > Ridge(ridge=1e-5)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item D. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge
model = Ridge(ridge=1e-5, previous=Reservoir(100, name="5", 
         previous=Reservoir(100, name="4", 
         previous=Reservoir(100, name="3", 
         previous=Reservoir(100, name="2", 
         previous=Reservoir(100, name="1"))))))
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{4. Write me an echo state network that can efficiently use the many CPU cores my machine has.}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. 
\begin{lstlisting}
import reservoirpy as rpy
rpy.set_param("backend", "parallel")

from reservoirpy.nodes import ESN
model = ESN(units=100)
model.fit(train_data, train_data)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item B. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.utils import parallel
from reservoirpy.nodes import ESN
model = ESN(units=100)
with parallel(n_jobs=-1):
    model.fit(train_data, train_data)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item C. 
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import ESN
model = ESN(units=100, workers=-1)
model.fit(train_data, train_data)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item D. ReservoirPy already parallelizes computation by default to ensure the best performance.
\end{itemize}

Correct: C

\subsection*{5. I have a model with several trainable readouts inside as such:}
\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge

model = Reservoir(100, name="R1") >> Ridge(name="readout1")
model >>= Reservoir(100, name="R2") >> Ridge(name="readout2")
model >>= Reservoir(100, name="R3") >> Ridge(name="readout3")
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{How can I fit the model, by specifying the Y values to each Ridge node?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. It is not possible to do such a thing in ReservoirPy as it wouldn't make sense.
  \item B. You can pass a dict as a y parameter: `model.fit(X, {"readout1": Y1, "readout2": Y2, "readout3": Y3, })`.
  \item C. You would have to fit each part separately before concatenating them.
  \item D. You can specify the node names as parameters and ReservoirPy will dispatch them correctly: `model.fit(X, readout1=Y1, readout2=Y2, readout3=Y3)`.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{6. I have a NumPy array \texttt{X} of shape \texttt{(timeseries, timesteps, dimensions)} and I want to classify them according to my \texttt{Y} array of shape \texttt{(timeseries, )} which contains numbers from 0 to 9 according to the class the timeseries belongs to. How can I do that in ReservoirPy?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A.
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, ScikitLearnNode, Ridge
from sklearn.linear_model import RidgeClassifier

Y_ = Y.reshape(-1, 1, 1).repeat(X.shape[1], 1)

model = Reservoir(1000, lr=0.9, sr=1.0) >> ScikitLearnNode(RidgeClassifier, model_hypers=dict(alpha=1e-8))
model.fit(X, Y_)
\end{lstlisting}

  \item B. Reservoir Computing is only a framework for timeseries forecasting, it is not suited for classification.

  \item C.
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, ScikitLearnNode, Ridge
from sklearn.linear_model import RidgeClassifier

model = Reservoir(1000, lr=0.9, sr=1.0) >> RidgeClassifier(alpha=1e-8)
model.fit(X, Y)
\end{lstlisting}

  \item D.
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, ScikitLearnNode, Ridge
from sklearn.linear_model import RidgeClassifier

Y_ = Y.reshape(-1, 1, 1).repeat(X.shape[1], 1)

model = Reservoir(1000, lr=0.9, sr=1.0) >> ScikitLearnNode(RidgeClassifier)
model.fit(X, Y_)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

