\subsection*{\textbf{Code questions (debug)}}

\subsection*{7. Here is my code:}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge

model = Reservoir(units=200, lr=0.2, sr=1.0) >> Ridge(ridge=1e-4)

for x_series, y_series in zip(X_train, Y_train):
    model.fit(x_series, y_series, warmup=10)

y_pred = model.run(X_test[0])
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{Is that correct?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. Calling \texttt{.fit} on a model erases the previous trained results. You can instead call \texttt{.fit} once by passing the lists \texttt{X\_train} and \texttt{Y\_train} as parameters.
  \item B. Everything is correct!
  \item C. \texttt{.fit} method is not suited for online training. Use \texttt{.train} instead.
  \item D. Reservoir parameters should be written in full form: \texttt{leak\_rate}, \texttt{spectral\_radius}.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

\subsection*{8. Here is my code:}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, Ridge

model = Reservoir(units=200, lr=0.2, sr=1.0, iss=0.2) >> Ridge(ridge=1e-4)

model.fit(X_train, Y_train, warmup=200)
Y_pred = model.run(X_test)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{I have an error. What's wrong?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. \texttt{iss} is not a parameter. For scaling the input, the correct parameter is \texttt{scale\_factor}.
  \item B. Reservoir parameters should be written in full form: \texttt{leak\_rate}, \texttt{spectral\_radius}, \texttt{input\_scaling}.
  \item C. You must first create the reservoir and readout nodes, and then connect them, in three separate lines.
  \item D. \texttt{iss} is not a parameter. For scaling the input, the correct parameter is \texttt{input\_scaling}.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{9. Here is my code:}


\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Reservoir, RLS

model = Reservoir(units=200, lr=0.2, sr=1.0) >> RLS(alpha=1e-4)

for x_series, y_series in zip(X_train, Y_train):
    model.fit(x_series, y_series, warmup=10)

y_pred = model.run(X_test[0])
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{I have an error. What's wrong?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. The RLS node can only be trained online, but the \texttt{.fit} method is for offline training. You should use \texttt{.train} instead.
  \item B. The model has been trained on a list of timeseries but is run on a single timeseries.
  \item C. There are not enough units inside the reservoir. For this task, having at least 1000 neurons is recommended.
  \item D. Wrong import path: to import the \texttt{Reservoir} node, use \texttt{from reservoirpy.nodes.reservoirs import Reservoir}.
\end{itemize}

Correct: A

\subsection*{10. Here's my code:}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
from reservoirpy.nodes import Input, Output, Reservoir, Ridge
R1 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.01, sr=1.)
R2 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.03, sr=1.)
R3 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.09, sr=1.)
R4 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.3, sr=1.)
R5 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.9, sr=1.)
R6 = Reservoir(100, lr=0.01, sr=1.)
readout = Ridge(ridge=1e-5, name="readout")

path1, path2 = R1 >> R6, R2 >> R5
path3 = Input(name="input") >> [R1, R2, R3]
path4 = R1 >> R2 >> R3 >> R4 >> R5 >> R6 >> readout >> Output()
model = path1 & path2 & path3 & path4

model.fit({"input": X_train}, {"readout": Y_train}, warmup=10)
model.run({"input": X_test})
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{Is that correct?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. The \texttt{.fit} and \texttt{.run} methods only take numpy arrays or list of numpy arrays, not dictionaries.
  \item B. Yes, everything is correct!
  \item C. There is a circular connection in the model.
  \item D. \texttt{path2} is not defined.
\end{itemize}

Correct: B

\subsection*{11. Is this the correct usage of the method \texttt{partial\_fit}?}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
reservoir, readout = Reservoir(100, sr=1), Ridge(ridge=1e-8)

for x, y in zip(X, Y):
    states = reservoir.run(x)
    readout.partial_fit(states, y)

readout.fit()
model = reservoir >> readout
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. By calling the method \texttt{.fit}, the readout forgets its previous training.
  \item B. The created model won't be fitted.
  \item C. While it works, it can be simplified by creating the model and calling \texttt{partial\_fit} on it.
  \item D. Yes, everything is correctly coded.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{12. Here is my code:}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
reservoir, readout = Reservoir(100, sr=1), Ridge(ridge=1e-8)

model = reservoir >> readout

model.fit(X[:800], Y[:800], warmup=10)

steps = 1000
results = np.zeros((steps, 1))

last_output = X[800]
for i in range(steps):
    last_output = model(last_output)
    results[i] = last_output
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{Is that the best way to have a model that generates new values by looping on itself?}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. No, you can connect the readout to the reservoir in order to loop the results back as an input after training: \texttt{readout >> reservoir}.
  \item B. No, it won't work as the reservoir has an input dimension of 100 and the \texttt{results} array containing the results only has its feature dimension set to 1.
  \item C. You can call the \texttt{.autoregress(n=1000)} Model method.
  \item D. Yes, this is probably the best solution.
\end{itemize}

Correct: D

\subsection*{13. Here is my code:}

\begin{itemize}[label= ]
    \item
\begin{lstlisting}
weights = np.random.choice([1, -1], p=[0.6, 1 - 0.6], replace=True, size=(200, 200))
reservoir = Reservoir(W=weights, sr=0.9, lr=0.6)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\textit{I created my reservoir this way, but it seems the reservoir has a very chaotic behavior, even though the spectral radius is below 1.}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A. The rule of the spectral radius <1 holds for matrices with a normal distribution, not a Bernoulli one, which explains why you have a chaotic behavior with a spectral radius of only 1.
  \item B. The rule of the spectral radius <1 only holds when there is no leak rate, so that explains why you have a chaotic behavior with a spectral radius of only 1.
  \item C. The parameter \texttt{sr} is only valid when no weight matrix has been specified. If a matrix is already specified, this argument is ignored.
  \item D. The reservoir argument names are incorrect. You should use \texttt{spectral\_radius} and \texttt{leak\_rate}.
\end{itemize}

Correct: C

